KANT_TR['busolt/kemp'] = {"busolt-s01-001":["Logic, or",[]],"busolt-s01-002":["the Doctrine of Reason [*Vernunftlehre*], by",[]],"busolt-s01-003":["Herr Professor Kant.",[]],"busolt-s01-004":["Königsberg, 8th Sept. 1790.",[]],"busolt-s01-005":["G. C. W. Busolt.",[]],"busolt-s01-006":["[Logik Busolt] Table of Contents",[]],"busolt-s01-007":["Prolegomena",[]],"busolt-s01-008":["608",[]],"busolt-s01-009":["The History of Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-010":["613",[]],"busolt-s01-011":["The Utility of Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-012":["613",[]],"busolt-s01-013":["Treatise of Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-014":["616-686",[]],"busolt-s01-015":["Of the Perfection of Knowledge in general",[]],"busolt-s01-016":["620",[]],"busolt-s01-017":["Of the Defects of Knowledge in general",[]],"busolt-s01-018":["622",[]],"busolt-s01-019":["Of the Horizon of Knowledge",[]],"busolt-s01-020":["623",[]],"busolt-s01-021":["Of the Misuse or Lack of Knowledge",[]],"busolt-s01-022":["626",[]],"busolt-s01-023":["Division of the Learned",[]],"busolt-s01-024":["627",[]],"busolt-s01-025":["Of Truth",[]],"busolt-s01-026":["627",[]],"busolt-s01-027":["Of the Clearness of Learned Knowledge",[]],"busolt-s01-028":["633",[]],"busolt-s01-029":["Of the Magnitude of Learned Knowledge",[]],"busolt-s01-030":["636",[]],"busolt-s01-031":["Of the Certainty of Learned Knowledge",[]],"busolt-s01-032":["637",[]],"busolt-s01-033":["Of the Various Kinds of Holding-to-be-true [*Fürwahrhalten*]",[]],"busolt-s01-034":["637",[]],"busolt-s01-035":["Of Hypotheses",[]],"busolt-s01-036":["647",[]],"busolt-s01-037":["Of Persuasion",[]],"busolt-s01-038":["647",[]],"busolt-s01-039":["Of Mathematical Certainty",[]],"busolt-s01-040":["649",[]],"busolt-s01-041":["Of Practical Modes of Knowledge",[]],"busolt-s01-042":["652",[]],"busolt-s01-043":["Of Concepts",[]],"busolt-s01-044":["653",[]],"busolt-s01-045":["Of Definitions",[]],"busolt-s01-046":["656",[]],"busolt-s01-047":["Of Philosophical Definition",[]],"busolt-s01-048":["657",[]],"busolt-s01-049":["The Logical Division of a Concept",[]],"busolt-s01-050":["660",[]],"busolt-s01-051":["Of Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-052":["661",[]],"busolt-s01-053":["The Division of Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-054":["661",[]],"busolt-s01-055":["Of Categorical Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-056":["664",[]],"busolt-s01-057":["Of Hypothetical and Disjunctive Judgments",[]],"busolt-s01-058":["666",[]],"busolt-s01-059":["Rules of Syllogisms",[]],"busolt-s01-060":["673",[]],"busolt-s01-061":["The Four Syllogisms according to their Figures",[]],"busolt-s01-062":["674",[]],"busolt-s01-063":["Rules of Syllogisms. Continuation",[]],"busolt-s01-064":["674",[]],"busolt-s01-065":["*De locis topicis* [Of the topical places]",[]],"busolt-s01-066":["681",[]],"busolt-s01-067":["Practical Logic",[]],"busolt-s01-068":["682",[]],"busolt-s01-069":["Remark on Meditating",[]],"busolt-s01-070":["685",[]],"busolt-s01-071":["Of the Use of Words",[]],"busolt-s01-072":["685",[]],"busolt-s01-073":["Logik Busolt",[]],"busolt-s01-074":["Prolegomena",[]],"busolt-s02-001":["Everything acts according to certain rules. These are either (1) universal or necessary rules, which are employed and required for every purpose, without special regard to this or that object; without them no employment of the understanding whatsoever is possible; or (2) particular rules, that is, such as have regard, for particular purposes, to this or that object. The former rules are unalterable; the latter are altered at will, for everything particular is contingent. Without the particular [rules] the employment of the understanding is possible only in a certain science. For the understanding is, properly speaking, the faculty of rules; the senses, on the other hand, are the faculty of intuition. In mathematics, for example, we can comprehend, without using our eyes or instruments, that a triangle has three sides and as many angles; but if we wish to know how a triangle is to be measured, the use of the eyes is necessarily required. The student learns mathematics as it can be comprehended through the understanding, the surveyor as it can be comprehended through the senses; and thus both act according to particular rules. Natural powers we observe before we know them; acquired powers we must know before we use them. The animals [and] inanimate nature act according to laws, according to rules, without being conscious of them. To man also they are often unknown, although he acts in accordance with them. We act according to natural rules:",[]],"busolt-s02-002":["(1) Because they already lie in nature.",[]],"busolt-s02-003":["(2) From imitation, where the practice takes place without knowledge of the rules.",[]],"busolt-s02-004":["(3) Through our own use and practice. Here we often err, but by this means we attain to proficiency, faults of habit excepted. Logic is the science of the rules of the employment of the understanding and of reason",[]],"busolt-s02-005":["Here below we find in ourselves the power of acting, and from this arises natural logic. For logic is the science of rules. In the beginning man acts according to rules of which he is not conscious; so long, therefore, he has a natural logic. When he comes to a riper age, he corrects this by rules, and so arises artificial logic. Thus someone speaks German and afterwards teaches himself the rules from a German grammar. Grammar, however, is only a discipline; logic is a science, a doctrine. The distinction between a discipline and a science or doctrine is this: in the former we cannot know why something is so and not otherwise, whereas in the latter we have an inner proof of it. In the former we consult experience, in the latter the understanding alone. The former is particular, the latter universal. The former is entitled *scientia empirica*, the latter *scientia scientifica*. Man has powers, some of which serve to provide materials, others to order them. The first is done by sensibility, the second by reason and the faculty of judgment.",[]],"busolt-s02-006":["Now artificial logic (for it is of this that we are here speaking) deals with universal rules, and therefore with such as must be employed in all sciences and in the judging of all objects. It accordingly separates off everything contingent and everything particular, and so abstracts also from the distinction between objects. It selects only the form, not the matter or content, of things. In every mode of knowledge we must distinguish the material, e.g. ‘If bodies are heavy, they are driven by others’, and the formal, e.g. ‘If God is just, He punishes evil actions’. Matter is the object of knowledge; form is the manner in which we know objects. Physics, for example, knows the objects of outer sense, psychology those of inner sense. Both we know empirically; this is the form, which is therefore the same in both. Logic, accordingly, considered in this way, is the science of the form of the understanding. Its rules are therefore *a priori*; that is, its universal rules have reason as their principle, whereas grammar and every other empirical science have rules *a posteriori*, that is, rules which have experience as their principle. Experience teaches us how a thing actually is; reason, how it must be, or whence it is so. That water, for instance, is fluid, experience teaches; but whence it is fluid, reason teaches. How men act morally we see from experience; how they ought to act we see from reason. All our modes of knowledge have two sources, the senses and the understanding; together they constitute the faculty of knowledge. This knowing is entitled *cognoscere*. The senses supply the matter for thought; the understanding thinks. The senses yield the representation, the understanding the rule. The rules of knowledge are either",[]],"busolt-s02-007":["(1) rules of sensibility, and this is entitled aesthetic, from αἰσθητός, *instrumentum sensorium* [organ of sense], or",[]],"busolt-s02-008":["(2) rules of the understanding, and this is entitled logic. This logic contains the rules of all modes of knowledge, and is distinguished from aesthetic in that in it everything is derived from reason and comprehended [through reason], whereas aesthetic is grounded on experience and reason. Logic is therefore entitled the doctrine of reason [*Vernunft-Lehre*], aesthetic the doctrine of taste [*Geschmacks-Lehre*].",[]],"busolt-s02-009":["All sciences which set forth rules are either (1) a canon, that is, a sum of rules *a priori* which teach us to judge whether something is in accordance with reason or not; or (2) an organon, that is, a sum of rules which serve for the perfecting of this or that science.",[]],"busolt-s02-010":["Is general logic, then, a canon or an organon? If it were to be an organon, it would have to give us directions how, for instance, mathematics or metaphysics might be brought to greater perfection. This, however, it does not do; its object is merely the form of the understanding, which must be the same in each and every object. Its object is therefore universal, and consequently it is not a science [in the strict sense], for every science has a particular object. Every science, moreover, extends our modes of knowledge, whereas logic only corrects them. General logic is therefore not an organon, nor a norm [*Norma*], for every norm is grounded on experience. It may, however, be entitled a norm *a priori*. An organon, further, is possible only when a science has already been discovered and brought to a certain height, so that it may now be perfected. Thus Bacon of Verulam wrote an *Organon scientiarum*, in which, among other things, he gives directions how physics might be brought to a high degree [of perfection]. Logic is rather a canon; that is, it contains rules which are grounded solely on the understanding, and which can be demonstrated only *a priori*. The examples in logic are taken from the common employment of the understanding in everyday life. It is therefore the propaedeutic of all the sciences. Aesthetic is not an organon, for it would then follow that one could learn to be witty; nor is it a canon, for it does not set forth rules *a priori*. It is a critique, or knowledge of rules, namely, of the most general rules for the cultivation of taste. It accordingly puts us in a position to judge a product of utility",[]],"busolt-s02-011":["properly. Remark: a canon is *doctrina demonstrabilis* [a demonstrable doctrine] and serves for *judication* [judging]; an organon serves for the *construction* of modes of knowledge. Consequently they [i.e. the rules of logic as canon] invent nothing; logic is therefore not *ars heuristica*, or the art of discovery. Yet it is of excellent service even to the inventive power, for it orders and alters it, and sifts what has been discovered. Aesthetic is the doctrine of the sensuous faculty of judgment, [deciding] whether a work of genius or anything else pleases or not. It is thus the sensuous faculty of knowledge, as taste is the sensuous faculty of judging. In matters of taste experiment and experience are always required; for although a piece may sometimes be composed according to all the rules of music, it nevertheless does not please. Rather, *plurima vota valent* [the majority of votes prevails]. Aesthetic, moreover, can contain only empirical rules, that is, rules *a posteriori*; and in this way one might say that it is a critique of the beautiful. For although everything here depends upon experience, the critic can nevertheless adduce grounds why this or that pleases or displeases. Yet there is something in what a poet says: *malo convivis quam coquis placere, fercula mea* [I would rather my dishes pleased the guests than the cooks].",[]],"busolt-s02-012":["In logic we must not intermix any alien science. Empirical psychology, for instance, does not belong to logic; for this is the doctrine of the phenomena of the human soul, that is, it sets forth general rules of the actual procedure of the human soul. Logic, above all, teaches not how we actually proceed but how we ought to proceed. This is indicated by the very word ‘rule’; for a rule is the condition under which something ought to be brought about. We divide the rules of the understanding into subjective rules, concerning the actual procedure of the understanding, and objective rules, concerning the correct rules according to which we ought to proceed. Logic is a doctrine of the universal laws of the understanding in general. It looks only to the form of the understanding in [dealing with] an object, not to the content of the object; for example, [it asks] whether an inference has been made according to the laws of inference. The logical employment of the understanding is either (1) common, and this is entitled *sensus communis*, *sana ratio*, *bon sens*; that is, by it we can comprehend something directly. This common understanding is the so-called average understanding. That is to say, if each man had the same amount of understanding, there would be exactly as much understanding among men as there now is, since it is distributed by nature so unequally. Each, however, has enough of it, and no one complains of a lack of understanding; for only with his own understanding can he measure the limits of understanding. In recent times appeal has been made to this sound understanding as if to a divine revelation, and men believe themselves entitled to exercise themselves no further in reflection and to take no further pains, since everyone can, it is held, easily see the matter for himself. Or (2) speculative. As to the distinction between the common and the speculative understanding, the point is that the speculative understanding knows things *in abstracto*, the common understanding *in concreto*. The rule of natural law, *casum sentit dominus* [the owner bears the loss], for instance, is understood by the speculative understanding without application to this or that particular case of everyday life; the common understanding, on the other hand, must first have examples and proofs. Remark: we can appeal to sound understanding only when something can be known *in concreto*. The speculative understanding must seek to accommodate itself to the common understanding; that is, it must make comprehensible *in concreto* what it has known *in abstracto*. This is entitled popularity, and it is precisely this which proves that our speculative knowledge is clear and distinct. In accordance with this twofold employment of the understanding, logic is divided into natural and speculative logic. Logic proper is the doctrine of the speculative employment of the understanding; for everyone already possesses natural logic of himself. It is also speculative for this reason, that it sets forth rules *in abstracto*.",[]],"busolt-s02-013":["Among the Aristotelians logic was divided into analytic and dialectic, that is, according to our author's explanation, into the logic (1) of certain and (2) of probable knowledge. But logic cannot in this way be divided with sufficient completeness; for on this view both have truth as their object, and they differ only in respect of certainty, the one being presented through insufficient, the other through sufficient grounds. The proper explanation of analytic and dialectic should be this: analytic is the logic of truth, dialectic the logic of illusion [*Schein*]. What, then, according to this explanation, is logic? Is it analytic or dialectic? Regarded as a canon it is an analytic; regarded as an organon, a dialectic. If logic, regarded as an organon, is taken for the logic of truth, the advocate becomes a pettifogger, the philosopher a sophist. For we then judge of objects according to universal rules without knowing the object itself, as, for instance, not long ago disputations, etc., were all set forth in mathematical order. The form was thus preserved, but the matter falsely presented. Exercise in the logic of illusion is nevertheless not to be censured. Logic is entitled the doctrine of reason, but not in respect of the order of its exposition; for every science must be expounded in accordance with reason. [It is so entitled] because it has reason for its object. Instead of the doctrine of reason it might also be called the doctrine of the understanding; for the understanding we employ in dealing with all objects, but not reason. We need not abstract entirely in logic; we may also make excursions into psychology and into rules *in concreto*, since logic is, properly, the doctrine of the borderland where reason adjoins sensibility, psychology and history. Logic is the canon of reason.",[]],"busolt-s03-001":["Aristotle was the first logician; he is the father of logic. His logic is a product of great acuteness. The Schoolmen adopted his doctrines and sought to exalt logic above all [other disciplines], but gained nothing thereby. Bacon of Verulam also took some pains with logic. Descartes did the same in his book *De methodo*. Malebranche in his work *Recherches de la vérité* writes more metaphysics than logic. Von Tschirnhaus writes a *Medicina mentis et corporis mathematice illustrata* [as the title is given in the transcript: *Medicinam mentis et corporis mathematice illustratur*], which is much to be recommended. Locke and Leibniz have also contributed [*verursacht*] a great deal to logic. Wolff's logic is the best. Most of these men did not set to logic its proper limits. It is, so to speak, the anatomy of the understanding. Crusius is also well regarded, but only by his adherents. Lambert's *Organon* contains a multitude of insights.",[]],"busolt-s04-001":["This [utility] is evident; for the form of the understanding is more important than the content, whereas a logical error is the very worst, since one not only commits it, but makes it a rule for oneself to err. Logic has, further, a general utility when it is expounded in popular fashion. All knowledge, accordingly, is divided",[]],"busolt-s04-002":["(1) into *cognitio ex datis*, i.e. *cognitio a posteriori*;",[]],"busolt-s04-003":["(2) into *cognitio ex principiis*, i.e. *cognitio a priori*, or knowledge of reason, *antequam nobis data sunt* [before (the objects) are given to us].",[]],"busolt-s04-004":["*Cognitio ex datis* is when an object is given to us, e.g. what we have from the narration of others, as for instance the motion of the stars. But whoever can state why an eclipse of the sun took place at precisely that time, proves [it] *ex principiis*. *Cognitio a posteriori* is empirical; *cognitio a priori* is speculative knowledge of reason. This latter we have either (1) from concepts, that is, philosophical knowledge;",[]],"busolt-s04-005":["(2) from construction, that is, mathematical knowledge. Mathematics and philosophy, therefore, differ greatly; for philosophy knows from concepts, mathematics from the construction of concepts. They are not, however, distinguished in respect of their object, for philosophy also has magnitudes as its object. Both are modes of knowledge of reason, and both are speculative; the difference lies in this, that the one rests on concepts, the other on the construction of concepts. If, for example, I wished to reflect upon a triangle, how great the angle would be which contained all three [angles] within itself, I should find out nothing philosophically by which to obtain a correct concept of it. I must therefore construct the concepts; that is, I must view the things *in singulari coram intuitu* [singly, before intuition]. *Intuitus a priori* is when I think and picture the figure to myself not from experience; *intuitus empiricus*, when I make the figure from experience. Mathematics has to do with *intuitus a priori*. It has been usual otherwise to distinguish philosophy and mathematics by their object, saying that philosophy treats of qualities, mathematics of quantities. This distinction, however, is incorrect; for both philosophy and mathematics treat of magnitudes, mathematics especially so, since only in respect of magnitude can I construct a concept. Philosophy is a science, and must constitute a system. It is therefore the system of all speculative knowledge from concepts. Our thinking is, properly, knowledge through concepts, or discursive knowledge. The senses, on the other hand, give us only knowledge through intuition, or intuitive modes of knowledge. He who possesses the former might be called a philosopher; he who has the latter, a man of sound understanding. Philosophy is not practical; it is the legislative science [*Gesezkunde*] of reason. The laws according to which we proceed are",[]],"busolt-s04-006":["(1) rules of skill, i.e. political laws;",[]],"busolt-s04-007":["(2) rules of wisdom, i.e. moral laws.",[]],"busolt-s04-008":["In philosophy we must distinguish objective and subjective knowledge. Objectively, this knowledge may be a knowledge of reason",[]],"busolt-s04-009":["*a priori*; subjectively, philosophy can be nothing but historical knowledge. Philosophy, then, knows *ex conceptu*, mathematics *ex intuitu*. In philosophy an *intuitus* can be only an example, whereas in mathematics an *intuitus* is what is essential. Wolff is therefore at fault when in his Epitome of all the Mathematical Sciences he gives a definition of similarity that is wholly speculative. Euclid proceeds quite otherwise, going at once to the application. Philosophical knowledge is accordingly discursive, mathematical knowledge intuitive. Philosophy cannot be learned by heart, and a philosopher cannot be the adherent of a particular sect; for he will then learn philosophy, not to philosophise. In mathematics, on the other hand, one can follow the utility and the necessity of another's theorem, since in mathematics, as an intuitive science, one can convince oneself, by the guiding-thread of intuition, whether this or that person has judged correctly; whereas in philosophy, as a discursive science, this guiding-thread is lacking. When we find *qui dicunt academia*, this signifies the Platonists; *qui dicunt porticus* signifies the Stoics; when we find *qui dicunt horti*, it signifies the Epicureans.",[]],"busolt-s04-010":["Philosophy is a speculative knowledge of reason according to rules *in abstracto*. It must be a system; that is, everything in it must be set forth in an orderly arrangement conforming to law. For it is through the architectonic [the transcript reads *Aesthetik*] that a system is distinguished from an aggregate, in which everything is thrown together in confusion, and which is also entitled a rhapsody.",[]],"busolt-s04-011":["Philosophy is a system of modes of knowledge of reason. Objectively, however, it can be a knowledge of reason and yet not be so subjectively. For even philosophy, which is a knowledge of reason in the strictest sense, can be learned from another by hearsay; and in that respect it is subjective, and accordingly not a knowledge of reason. A philosopher is either practical, that is, one who through concepts is master of his passions, or theoretical, one who is the lawgiver, or rather the one versed in the laws, of human reason. The natural scientist [*Naturlehrer*] is not a philosopher, for he employs reason, so far as he can, upon natural objects; whereas the metaphysician lets his reason, as it were, contemplate itself, and therefore rightly bears the name of philosopher. He is a *legislator* of humanity; a sophist, on the other hand, is its *leguleius* [pettifogger].",[]],"busolt-s05-001":["Here we are speaking of knowledge in general. Logic itself treats of representations, not, however, of those of which we are not conscious, that is, obscure representations, but of clear ones, that is, of those of which we have consciousness. Every mode of knowledge has an object, and can therefore be considered in a twofold manner, namely:",[]],"busolt-s05-002":["(1) In relation to the object; this is the material [element].",[]],"busolt-s05-003":["(2) In relation to the form of the understanding, that is, the formal [element] of knowledge.",[]],"busolt-s05-004":["In logic we abstract from the object and look to the form of the understanding, that is, the *modus cognoscendi*. This formal [element] of knowledge is again:",[]],"busolt-s05-005":["(1) *Logicum*: thinking, intellectual;",[]],"busolt-s05-006":["(2) *Aestheticum*: intuiting, sensuous.",[]],"busolt-s05-007":["Representations are:",[]],"busolt-s05-008":["(1) Subjective, i.e. sensation; it is the alteration of the state in the subject.",[]],"busolt-s05-009":["(2) Objective, i.e. knowledge; it refers [*reflectirt*] the representation to the object.",[]],"busolt-s05-010":["Sensation can be communicated only to one who has or has had it. Consequently nothing regular can be established concerning it either in logic or in aesthetic, since one subject always remains distinct from others, and so too, therefore, does the sensation. Every representation is always knowledge as intuition [or as concept]. Representations are thus",[]],"busolt-s05-011":["(1) of intuition (sensibility),",[]],"busolt-s05-012":["(2) of concepts (understanding).",[]],"busolt-s05-013":["(1) Intuition refers to a single object, in so far as it is distinguished from others.",[]],"busolt-s05-014":["(2) Concepts refer to something universal, for they make the particular universal. From these divisions there then arise:",[]],"busolt-s05-015":["(a) Aesthetic, that is, the discipline which sets forth the rule of perfection from the representations of intuition.",[]],"busolt-s05-016":["(b) Logic is the science which sets forth the rules of perfection from the representations of the understanding.",[]],"busolt-s05-017":["Logic, accordingly, has to do with clear representations, that is, with such concepts as we are conscious of; and these we again divide into distinct and indistinct.",[]],"busolt-s05-018":["A distinct concept is one by which we know the manifold that a thing contains. In the concept of virtue, for example, I think three things:",[]],"busolt-s05-019":["(1) Conformity to rule.",[]],"busolt-s05-020":["(2) Freedom to act.",[]],"busolt-s05-021":["(3) Mastery over desires and passions.",[]],"busolt-s05-022":["If we have a concept merely of the whole, but not of the parts, the concept is entitled clear but indistinct. It is not properly called confused, for confusion is opposed to order, not to distinctness. All modes of knowledge are attained through order, that is, *per reductionem idearum sub actis quosdam titulos* [by the reduction of ideas under certain headings]. Between distinct and confused there is yet a middle term, namely simple. A simple representation is one which contains no manifold. Of a single book, for example, I cannot say that it lies in disorder. Distinctness of intuition is not possible without concepts of the parts. – – Distinctness of concepts is possible only if we at the same time know and distinguish the manifold. Philosophy is concerned to hold our understanding to the task of analysing our universal concepts and showing us the whole store of our knowledge. *Est enim, ut aiebat Socrates, philosophia cognitionum nostrarum quasi quaedam obstetrix, non mater* [For philosophy, as Socrates said, is as it were the midwife, not the mother, of our modes of knowledge].",[]],"busolt-s05-023":["Thus in philosophy we learn the distinction between pride, arrogance and haughtiness [*Stolz, Hochmuth, Hoffarth*], although we had already previously acquired some concepts of them. But in philosophy obscure concepts become clear, and clear concepts distinct; and in it we are, as it were, made conscious of our knowledge. The distinction between logical and aesthetic representations consists in this:",[]],"busolt-s05-024":["(1) Aesthetic representation is representation through the senses or the imagination [*Einbildungskraft*]. This *imagination* belongs, as it were, to the senses; it is their copyist; it is the source of intuition.",[]],"busolt-s05-025":["(2) Logical representation is representation through the understanding; and thinking is therefore entitled knowing through concepts.",[]],"busolt-s05-026":["The first kind of representation is also called the sensuous faculty of knowledge, or the lower power of the soul; the second kind, on the other hand, the higher power of the soul.",[]],"busolt-s05-027":["Reason stands in the same relation to the understanding as the understanding to the senses. Both kinds are required for a concept, and in no other way can a concept be produced. Aesthetic perfections are either essential, that is, the conformity of the sensuous with the logical, or intellectual: a piece cannot please, even with the wittiest conceits, if one cannot find the agreement of the whole play of imagination with the theme. It is precisely from this essential perfection that the *sensus publicus*, universal taste, arises; for this is the faculty of choice according to the universal laws of sensibility. Or, [on the other hand,] aesthetic perfections are contingent. These cannot be determined, since they concern the subject.",[]],"busolt-s05-028":["The universal and the particular laws of aesthetic.",[]],"busolt-s05-029":["Universal laws are possible in respect of intuition. For in judging of the beautiful, which is investigated in aesthetic (as the true is in logic), intuition is the principal thing, sensation the *accessorium*. Intuition can be shared [reading *mitgeteilt* for *geteilt*?]; sensations, however, cannot be imparted; if this is done, it is done through similitudes. Private laws rest on the differing sensations of each individual. Sensations are certain alterations of our state, whereby all our powers of knowledge are enlivened and exerted. Taste is the faculty of the sensuous power of judging according to universal laws of sensibility. Feeling is the faculty of sensation according to private laws of sensibility.",[]],"busolt-s05-030":["From this arises the mixture of our modes of knowledge out of sensations and concepts. If our knowledge is separated from all sensations, it is pure. The purer the knowledge, the less is it sensation; the stronger the sensation, the less correct and pure is the knowledge. We must not, therefore, let ourselves be carried away by the dazzling enchantment of the senses, but must first ground the knowledge itself and then build the sensation upon it; and in this way there arises proper edification. Sensations take us by surprise; knowledge comes by degrees. The former are something transitory, the latter something abiding. General aesthetic perfection consists in charm and emotion [*Reiz und Rührung*]. (*Delinimentum est, mentis concussio.*) *Attrahit delinimentum, nos quoad contemplandum longius objectum detinet. Convolvit concussio animum nobisque quasi quendam horrorem infundit. Mens delinimenta sentit, quandam animae voluptatem, atque in objecto quasi acquiescit. Concussio est tamquam animae aliqua actio. Delinimento, ne ob dulce animis condimentum fastidium moveat, concussionis atque commutationis aliquid admisceatur; conquassatio haec est, habetur quidam dolor, puncto temporis inquinatam voluptatem resolvitur.* [Charm attracts, and holds us to the longer contemplation of the object. Emotion stirs up the mind and pours into us, as it were, a kind of shudder. The mind feels charms, a certain pleasure of the soul, and as it were comes to rest in the object. Emotion is, as it were, an activity of the soul. With charm, lest by its sweet seasoning it arouse disgust in the mind, something of emotion and change should be mingled. This is a shaking: a certain pain is felt, and in a moment the pleasure, thus tainted, is dissolved.]",[]],"busolt-s05-031":["Of the various kinds of modes of knowledge. A rational knowledge [*vernünftige Erkenntnis*] and a knowledge of reason [*Vernunfterkenntnis*] are very different. A knowledge is rational when it is merely in accordance with reason, that is, such as a man can comprehend through his reason; modes of knowledge of reason are those which are derived from reason. In every science such are required. The author says that a learned knowledge and a philosophical knowledge are one and the same; but they are very different. For all our knowledge is either historical or knowledge of reason; and the latter, if it is to be philosophy, must be speculative, from concepts. To historical knowledge belongs literature [*Litteratur*]; this comes from *litterator*, that is, one who knows books. Such a one is a philologist if he is a critic and knows especially the ancient languages. It is already a high degree of such learning, [rising] up to the polyhistor, who is, as it were, a giant of erudition. He does not learn philosophy and mathematics; he knows them already historically. He has a knowledge of books, and passes judgment even on the ancient philosophers. They are nothing but half-learned; they know historically what great men have said, but the ground is lacking to them. By the path of imitation I cannot become a philosopher. I must owe nothing to others, but only improve [my] concepts through theirs. A philosopher is not a learned man [*Gelehrter*], for in philosophy one has no need to be instructed. A learned man is one who has been taught [*gelehrt*]. Such men know nothing other than what they have been taught. Philosophy and mathematics, however, I can have from myself; only history can I learn. A learned man is *eruditus*, not *litteratus*; the latter is one who can read and write. Mathematics is not erudition but a knowledge of reason; philosophy too is a knowledge of reason. In philosophy, however, one speaks discursively, in mathematics intuitively. A mathematical chatterer is not conceivable, but a philosophical one certainly is, namely when one learns the expressions which all [philosophers] have used and adds something new. Such men make a show of the sciences. The fine arts and letters [*schöne Wissenschaften*] are not erudition. It is good if a poet or an orator is a learned man; but what is beautiful, what moves, he must have from reason. They are arts, not sciences. What matters in them is not knowing something but being able to do something. Wit, taste and genius are required. Aesthetic is a critique of art. Logical perfection makes the particular universal, and proceeds from intuition to concepts; aesthetic perfection, on the other hand, proceeds conversely. The greatest perfection is the combination of logical with aesthetic perfection, since only then does one comprehend and become intelligible. All knowledge which concerns our understanding refers to the object; all knowledge which concerns our senses refers to the subject. Logic aims at knowing the particular through universal concepts. Aesthetic knows the universal through the particular; it merely fills the soul with intuitions. Logic seeks to bring all knowledge to concepts, aesthetic to bring all to intuitions. The two must be capable of being united if I am to know the universal *in concreto*. Only he can make knowledge sensuous who makes it intuitive. A knowledge which is logically but not aesthetically perfect is called thorough, but dry. On the other hand, if a knowledge is beautiful, stimulating, agreeable, engaging, but lacks logical perfection, it is shallow. What entertains does not, indeed, constitute the essential; but our knowledge nevertheless loses if it is lacking. To beauty is opposed, on the one hand, the lack of it, and on the other, ugliness. The first is called dryness. Affectation is an ugliness. One can narrate the most horrible representations and yet clothe them beautifully; only not what is disgusting, for this can be concealed by no art.",[]],"busolt-s06-001":["Knowledge is the name given to the whole extent of the concept which one has attained of a thing, and which comprises all the propositions concerning it that have become known to him. The greater the number of these propositions, the more detailed and complete is our knowledge; the more rigorously we can prove them, the more certain; and the more frequently these propositions admit of application to cases that arise, the more useful. The perfection of knowledge is specified according to quality and quantity. The perfection of knowledge in respect of quality is the same in all modes of knowledge; in respect of quantity, everything depends, in the case of each object, on the amount and the degree. In respect of quality the perfection of knowledge is theoretical and practical. The first relates to the understanding, the second to the will. The first is considered only in logic; the second determines actions.",[]],"busolt-s06-002":["Theoretical knowledge is the fruit of speculation or reflection; practical knowledge grows out of the applications which one finds occasion to make in practice. The former is obtained by laying certain concepts at the foundation, drawing consequences from them, and then setting inference upon inference; the latter consists in a collection of experiences and of consequences drawn from them. Theoretical perfection is either aesthetic or logical.",[]],"busolt-s06-003":["(1) Logical perfection contains",[]],"busolt-s06-004":["(a) the matter (*objectum*), which consists in truth, and is thus [perfection] in respect of quality;",[]],"busolt-s06-005":["(b) the form (*modum cognoscendi*), which consists",[]],"busolt-s06-006":["(aa) in universality, in that we know everything from concepts;",[]],"busolt-s06-007":["(bb) in distinctness, in that we represent these abstract concepts *in concreto*.",[]],"busolt-s06-008":["(2) Aesthetic perfection contains",[]],"busolt-s06-009":["(a) the material [element], which is here sensation;",[]],"busolt-s06-010":["(b) the formal [element], that is",[]],"busolt-s06-011":["aa.) Manifoldness [Mannigfaltigkeit]. Logical universality is the opposite of this. In its definitions logical universality must dismiss everything else and abbreviate everything, whereas manifoldness extends and enlarges everything, by synonyms, etc., etc.",[]],"busolt-s06-012":["bb.) Unity: for all these enlargements must still together make up a whole. For example, the description of spring from the mouth of the poet and from that of the astronomer.",[]],"busolt-s06-013":["Universality is what is essential in concepts; manifoldness is what is essential in intuition. Aesthetic perfection in respect of form pairs best with logic. Logical perfection combined with aesthetic perfection is entitled cogency [Bündigkeit]. Aesthetic and logic combined is entitled manifoldness. Manifoldness and universality are so combined that neither does injury to the other",[]],"busolt-s06-014":["Distinctness in logic is what liveliness is in aesthetic. The livelier the representation, the more emotion [Rührung] it arouses, but the less I know [erkenne]. Liveliness is thus the quickening of the senses. Distinctness, on the other hand, occupies us with the object.",[]],"busolt-s06-015":["Aesthetic perfection requires:",[]],"busolt-s06-016":["1.) taste, and this is the form;",[]],"busolt-s06-017":["2.) feeling, and this is the matter: for charm and emotion belong to it.",[]],"busolt-s06-018":["A practical perfection of logic is not possible, for it would be the movement of the will, and this is treated in moral philosophy. The art of the beautiful is sometimes appropriate. But to employ in poetry the picturesque beauty of nature is not appropriate. For no art attains the beauties of nature by depicting and describing them. We seem to wish, as it were, to compete with nature, [to see] whether we too can produce an intuitive concept that may keep pace with nature; and that is why Haller is censured for his Swiss poems. In respect of human actions, on the other hand, art can not only attain them but even surpass them. In the former [i.e. the beauties of nature], for example, our depicting cannot make us behold so much as intuition does; as when Milton magnificently described the globe and its fall. We may not venture with picturesque beauties as far as the Supreme Being. An astronomer could therefore depict God better than a poet. For the former says: Behold, so great are the works of the Lord; whereas the latter, with his sensuous images, would depict Him too lowly, since sensibility cannot be thought in the concept of God.",[]],"busolt-s07-001":["Defects (*defectus*) must be carefully distinguished from faults (*vitiis*). In the case of a defect the knowledge is still correct; it is merely lacking to me. In the case of a fault, however, it is erroneous. For example, in geography I may leave out important cities; this is a defect. But if I locate a place wrongly, this is a fault. A defect can be made good; a fault, however, must first be reduced to a defect. An error is a hindrance to true knowledge. Rather than become confirmed in error, therefore, one should prefer to remain at a certain degree of ignorance.",[]],"busolt-s08-001":["A horizon in general is a circle which bounds all the objects that we can see. Here the horizon is: *congruentia cognitionis cum terminis perfectionis humanae* [the congruence of knowledge with the limits of human perfection]. This horizon is divided into the logical, according to the faculty of knowledge, and the practical, according to utility. The determination of the logical horizon must precede that of the practical, for utility follows only upon knowledge. Here I must not ask first about utility. Uses often remain hidden for a very long time. When Archimedes discovered the ratio of the cone to the cylinder, he would have found it difficult to state its use; only long afterwards did it show itself in ordinary life. The practical part does not belong to logic, and nothing more will therefore be said of it. As regards the logical horizon, we divide it:",[]],"busolt-s08-002":["1.) into the universal horizon of the human understanding in general. This can be determined only through profound metaphysical inquiries.",[]],"busolt-s08-003":["2.) into the practical horizon of this or that purpose. Here the following distinction is to be noted:",[]],"busolt-s08-004":["a.) how I determine the horizon of my",[]],"busolt-s08-005":["b.) how I determine the horizon of the knowledge [Kenntnisse] of others. I can determine the horizon",[]],"busolt-s08-006":["1.) from the purposes of life,",[]],"busolt-s08-007":["2.) from sex,",[]],"busolt-s08-008":["3.) from station,",[]],"busolt-s08-009":["4.) from a man's age.",[]],"busolt-s08-010":["Rules in determining the horizon. One should not at the outset determine the horizon too widely. In school, for example, one learns many things which one afterwards lets lie, such as the oriental languages. One should therefore always determine the horizon narrowly. But neither should one determine one's horizon altogether too narrowly, because",[]],"busolt-s08-011":["a.) much of [our] knowledge [Kenntnisse] is lost,",[]],"busolt-s08-012":["b.) because we do not know to what use we may yet put these modes of knowledge.",[]],"busolt-s08-013":["One should not change the horizon of one's knowledge too often, as when, for example, a theologian becomes a jurist, and sometimes conversely. The universal logical horizon we can also determine:",[]],"busolt-s08-014":["1.) logically or *a priori*, and this is the horizon of reason;",[]],"busolt-s08-015":["2.) empirically or *a posteriori*:",[]],"busolt-s08-016":["aa.) in respect of individual objects;",[]],"busolt-s08-017":["bb.) in respect of the whole human race, namely, how far the sciences have advanced from the beginning down to our own times.",[]],"busolt-s08-018":["The first, namely the horizon of reason, is determined especially in metaphysics; for there one asks, not how far the human understanding has got, but how far it could get. The horizon of reason is:",[]],"busolt-s08-019":["a.) the horizon of sound reason, and",[]],"busolt-s08-020":["b.) the horizon of speculative reason.",[]],"busolt-s08-021":["The horizon of sound reason is more narrowly bounded than that of speculative reason. Everything is advancing: physics, natural history, mathematics, history; and it will necessarily be needful to cast everything into a more compact form, lest the sciences fall into decay merely because they have risen so high. For men would necessarily be discouraged if they could not hope to make the name of a discoverer illustrious. In the present age, therefore, much philosophy is needed in order to choose, from so many sciences, those that are most necessary and most useful. To recognise ignorance one must know much, for the ignorant man does not recognise his ignorance. He cannot comprehend how he should be lacking so much in knowledge of the world. The common man, for example, takes the stars to be points, and believes that others also take them to be so. The more we know, the more we see that we are still ignorant, and that of the great field of possible and useful sciences we cultivate only a small part. To know one's ignorance one must know at least an outline of the sciences, so that we may recognise what we know and what we lack; we must know the sciences at least extensively. Ignorance in and by itself is something blameworthy; but not every ignorance is to be regretted. For he who is always digging behind the actions of men and searching out their sources will in the end, if he everywhere finds only self-interest, one's own advantage and one's own purposes as the motives of actions, hold virtue to be a chimera and its practice folly. No one has ever possessed perfect virtue, nor can it be practised. Scholars who know their ignorance not infrequently become confused; for example, Condamine, the celebrated French naturalist, who, on beholding the inexhaustible store of natural things in Peru, lost all desire to extend natural history. These [natural things] will set us ever more problems (*problemata*) which we cannot solve. Of the extent of knowledge",[]],"busolt-s08-022":["1.) Erudition [Gelehrsamkeit] is the sum of all the historical sciences.",[]],"busolt-s08-023":["2.) The philology of the ancient languages is the sum of the instruments of erudition.",[]],"busolt-s08-024":["A philologist is:",[]],"busolt-s08-025":["a.) a *litterator*, that is, one who has a bibliographical knowledge of the ancient writings;",[]],"busolt-s08-026":["b.) a linguist, if he knows the ancient languages, and that critically;",[]],"busolt-s08-027":["c.) a humanist, if he studies the beauty of the ancient writings; and then",[]],"busolt-s08-028":["d.) a *belletrist*, who forms his taste from the more recent writings.",[]],"busolt-s08-029":["Polyhistory as a whole seems to be an excessively great knowledge. But it is only a knowledge of all the sciences. One might call it a Cyclops, because it lacks an eye, namely critical philosophy. Philosophy is also a polyhistory, but only historical philosophy. Alongside polyhistory is placed polymathy. The former is a knowledge of all the historical sciences, the latter a knowledge of all the sciences of reason, or of all the opinions of the philosophers. A scholar who is both together is a pansophist.",[]],"busolt-s08-030":["But this requires more memory than genius. Encyclopaedic skill is a facility in reducing the sciences to small compass. It is rhapsodic and a mere compilation; but it ought to be a system, and must therefore be not rhapsodic but methodical. The encyclopaedia brings all the sciences under one title; it gives their genealogical tree. Such a tree is organic. The dead languages are the vehicle of polyhistory and the medium of commerce of the fine sciences [schöne Wissenschaften], because they",[]],"busolt-s08-031":["1.) are the instrument of the sciences, and",[]],"busolt-s08-032":["2.) do not change.",[]],"busolt-s09-001":["To this belong pedantry and charlatanry. The word pedant comes from the Italian. A *magister pedarius* was one who conducted children and was solely in their company, and who consequently adopted manners that do not suit public life. For such men commonly observe an altogether too anxious punctiliousness in formulas; to this is opposed a purposive exactness. Charlatanry borders on pedantry. For he who possesses the former has, indeed, no knowledge such as the schools require, but nevertheless seeks to deceive others by the semblance of possessing it. Pedantry, on the other hand, presupposes knowledge such as the schools require. Charlatans approach popularity especially, in that they present knowledge which not infrequently demands deep reflection as though it were play, and as easily comprehensible to everyone's power of apprehension. This is a chief characteristic of the French. The author says that pedantry is a charlatanry. But the two are very different. The former is a learned but restricted knowledge without popularity. The latter is a boastfulness in knowledge, and consists in seeking to bring one's own restricted skill to bear everywhere. The perfection of knowledge must have a certain art of relating [itself]. It is either:",[]],"busolt-s09-002":["a.) scholastic, if it forms part of erudition, or *coascendentia ad certum conditionis gradum* [ascent to a certain degree of condition], or",[]],"busolt-s09-003":["b.) popular, that is, condescension to general concepts.",[]],"busolt-s09-004":["This condescension is a skill in accommodating oneself",[]],"busolt-s09-005":["aa.) both to the sum [of what is to be known], and also",[]],"busolt-s09-006":["bb.) to the capacity and power of comprehension of the *publicum* and of the multitude.",[]],"busolt-s09-007":["Scholastic knowledge may be great in degree and small in application. If one wishes to be popular, one must deduct something from thoroughness, completeness and determinateness. Scholastic knowledge has thoroughness as its essential feature; popularity adorns and embellishes; only thoroughness, solidity, is led in the go-cart of terminology. A sermon must be not only scholastically correct but also popular, and this requires a sustained condescension. A preacher cannot be a polished orator. For the more dignity a thing itself has, the less ornament it needs. In general, solidity is always to be preferred to popularity; for the more popular, the less solid. The skill of accommodating oneself to all the world is a knowledge of the world, and one who possesses it is entitled a scholar of the world. Pedantry is thus the lack of popularity in learned knowledge.",[]],"busolt-s10-001":["1.) Into guild scholars, who seek their bread through erudition.",[]],"busolt-s10-002":["2.) Into scholars free of the guild, who use erudition merely for pleasure.",[]],"busolt-s10-003":["The former must necessarily be of more use to the world than the latter. For they find themselves compelled to reflect more deeply on the matter, since they are obliged to show their hearers the object, as it were, in various positions. The others are certainly prouder; for they say that the liberal arts ought not to be used with the aim [of making them] trades.",[]],"busolt-s11-001":["Truth is the agreement of knowledge with the object. Illusion [Schein] is contradictorily opposed to truth. On the question of universal criteria the philosopher founders. Philosophers have been unable to find any universal criterion of truth. A universal criterion is one in which a mark is supplied without regard to the object itself. It must be indifferent to all objects; the explanation must here abstract from all differences of determinate objects, and it is therefore concerned solely with the form. The universal criterion of truth thus already lies in the question. He [who asks] wants signs of truth, and this is the agreement of knowledge with the object. He wants universal marks; that is, he wants a sign which applies universally to all objects, a sign of the form. The universal criterion of truth is here, therefore, merely formal: the agreement of knowledge with the object. Logic can therefore give only formal, not material, universal criteria of truth. It indicates merely a relative criterion; universal material criteria of truth logic cannot give. For a mode of knowledge may be false in relation to one object and true in relation to another. Every object has its material criterion. The material criterion cannot hold of all objects. Material truth consists solely in the distinguishing of one object from others. He, therefore, who demands of the logician a universal criterion of truth must not demand a knowledge of the object, [as to] whether it is correct; nor [can he demand] a universal criterion of truth [in that sense]. The universal criterion of truth in logic is an agreement of knowledge with itself. This mark indirectly supplies a distinction: if a mode of knowledge is contrary to the logical form, it is certainly false, because it contradicts itself.",[]],"busolt-s11-002":["Remark. The dialectical employment of the understanding is the production of illusion. The formal very often deceives. A fine coat, for example, adorns a dullard, whereas a scholar is often hidden under a shabby coat; and so it is with modes of knowledge. If of two opposed propositions the one is false, the other is true. He, therefore, who wishes to show the truth of a proposition has two ways before him. Either he can prove directly the truth of the proposition itself, or he can prove the falsity of its opposite. The latter is done when he correctly infers from the opposite something absurd; for a proposition is certainly false if it has false consequences. In the first case the proof is entitled a direct demonstration, in the other an indirect or apagogic demonstration. The criteria of truth are either essential or extra-essential. They are essential when they rest in the knowledge itself and accord with the supreme laws of the understanding; extra-essential when they consist in agreement with the common human understanding. The marks which we have of truth are thus twofold:",[]],"busolt-s11-003":["1.) *a priori*, and",[]],"busolt-s11-004":["2.) *a posteriori*.",[]],"busolt-s11-005":["The judging *a posteriori*, whether a proposition which I have made according to universal rules of the understanding also finds assent with the understanding of everyone, is something we cannot dispense with. It is the test whether a proposition does or does not accord with the common laws of the understanding. [These] marks are not objective but subjective; they are derived from the agreement of other, particular subjects. There are, accordingly, objective criteria of truth; these consist in the agreement of knowledge with the laws of the understanding and of reason. These are not sufficient. There are two subjective criteria of truth which must be added to the former, and which consist in taking together the judgments of different people. Here, therefore, I am concerned with the agreement of subjects. –",[]],"busolt-s11-006":["I must make a test:",[]],"busolt-s11-007":["1.) I compare the knowledge which I have of objects with the knowledge of other objects which has already been accepted as true. This makes a great difference in the application of the common rules of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"busolt-s11-008":["2.) I see whether the knowledge of my subject agrees with the knowledge of other subjects.",[]],"busolt-s11-009":["In general, that is true which holds for everyone; what has only a private validity is mere illusion. The impulse, in investigating truth, to take counsel with others is already implanted in man by nature. The formal, essential marks [Kenntnisse] of truth are:",[]],"busolt-s11-010":["a.) A mode of knowledge must be possible; this is a negative criterion.",[]],"busolt-s11-011":["b.) A mode of knowledge must in a certain manner be grounded:",[]],"busolt-s11-012":["A.) as consequence, and",[]],"busolt-s11-013":["B.) as ground. As consequence, if it is a correct consequence from true grounds; as ground, if it is a ground of true consequences.",[]],"busolt-s11-014":["Consequences can be regarded as grounds (*rationes*), not indeed as *rationes succedendi*, but as *rationes cognoscendi*. The possibility of the knowledge must be connected with a true ground which has correct consequences, and with true consequences which have a correct ground. – Groundlessness and impossibility are signs of falsity. Groundlessness is an incomplete sign of falsity. – Impossibility is a sufficient, apodictic ground that a mode of knowledge is false. In the case of truth, on the other hand, possibility is a complete sign of it, but groundedness a sufficient sign.",[]],"busolt-s11-015":["Truth is a perfection of knowledge in general. It belongs, however, specifically to logical knowledge. It is logically and also aesthetically perfect, but especially aesthetically; for it relates to the subject.",[]],"busolt-s11-016":["Logical and aesthetic truth differ in this: aesthetic truth is that which agrees with the ends of sensibility. One of the two may be found in a mode of knowledge without the other. Shakespeare, for example, is censured for letting ghosts appear. Here there is, indeed, no logical truth, but there is aesthetic truth; for Shakespeare's purpose is to impress this rightly upon the senses. Moreover, it is not at all impossible that spirits might appear to us sensibly, although it is not established. For these purposes of Shakespeare, however, aesthetic truth suffices. So too, in Aesop's fables, the animals are represented as speaking. We can think a mode of knowledge that is wholly true, one also that is wholly false, but also one that is neither true nor false. Every judgment, even if it is false, must yet in some respect accord with the laws of the understanding, and so be in some degree true. It is therefore certain that in a false judgment there is always some small element of truth, even if it is inconsiderable compared with what is false. The author draws a distinction between exact and crude knowledge. This distinction is incorrect. For it is subtle knowledge that is opposed to crude. An exact knowledge is one that agrees with the object in all respects and is suited to the end. It is entitled precise knowledge. Subtle knowledge is that in which there is a greater degree of knowledge than the end requires; crude knowledge, that in which there is a lesser degree of knowledge than the end requires. For example, the knowledge of God is [in different men] an exact, a crude, and also a subtle knowledge. All men must have a correct knowledge of God. The degree of correctness differs in different men. A teacher, for example, must indeed have a subtle knowledge. If, however, he were to think of Him as a corporeal being, that is, if he had a crude knowledge, he would on that account be greatly to blame, even though this is permitted to the common man. Subtle knowledge errs in extension. There are sciences which demand everything with precision. For example, [if] I go for a walk, it is better that I give to the poor the money which I intended to spend on it; thereby I fulfil my duty of love. One says that something is true *tolerabiliter* [tolerably], that is, when the error in the knowledge is not considerable, or when what is incorrect in a mode of knowledge does not hinder its end. In § 104 the author draws a distinction between dogmatic and historical truths (*ex datis*). It is the latter that are opposed to the knowledge of reason (*ex principiis*), not the dogmatic. Knowledge of reason is from concepts, and is an apodictic ground, that is, one which with the consciousness of necessity",[]],"busolt-s11-017":["is combined. Knowledge *ex principiis* is either from concepts or from the construction of concepts. Modes of knowledge from concepts are *dogmata*; those which are combined with intuition, that is, in which the universal is known in intuition (*in concreto*), are *mathemata*. The former, therefore, is philosophical, the latter mathematical knowledge. Now for the author [a system is] a number of dogmatic judgments, truths so connected that they make up a whole. But this is not a system. For one can take the manifold together through mere similarity. A system, however, is where the idea of the whole precedes the determination of the parts; an aggregate, where the knowledge of the parts precedes the concept of the whole. Our author's explanation of a system would thus be nothing other than [the explanation of] an aggregate. I can aggregate according to similarity, not according to ideas. The author says that a system consists of dogmatic truths. This is not right, for there are also other systems; the system of nature, for example, is a historical system. All knowledge that makes up an aggregate may be entitled a rhapsody; that which makes up a system, a science, that is, a concept of the whole, wherein the parts taken together make up a whole. Truth is opposed to error. Error is a representation of a false mode of knowledge as a truth. We do not always make use of the laws of understanding and of reason. How can error arise? This is a difficult question. We therefore note:",[]],"busolt-s11-018":["1.) Error and truth are found only in reason, not in concepts or intuitions.",[]],"busolt-s11-019":["2.) Through the senses we have no judgments, but only intuition. Where there is no judging, there is neither truth nor error. The senses, therefore, do not err.",[]],"busolt-s11-020":["3.) Nor does the understanding err in [the case of] concepts",[]],"busolt-s11-021":["A compendious means of not erring is not to judge at all. This means is not, indeed, to be commended; nevertheless it is well [to employ it] when the knowledge of the thing [nicht] too far transcends our knowledge by the understanding, since error then very easily arises. One can judge and yet not err, namely, if one judges not decisively, not assertorically but problematically; if I add to my judgment: *non liquet*, it is not established. But to judge in this way is no gain for reason. I must only at the beginning judge the knowledge problematically, and in time assertorically. Problematic judging must precede assertoric. Those who judge problematically are entitled doubters; they might better be called *cunctatores* [delayers].",[]],"busolt-s11-022":["All judgments are actions of the understanding and of reason; and although they may also be erroneous, we cannot say that the understanding errs in itself. For it is the touchstone of truth. The understanding always acts in accordance with its laws, and this accordance is formal truth. The senses do not err in themselves, for they do not judge. Error arises from the mixing of the two; for we do not distinguish the inference of the senses from the effects of the understanding. A power can deviate from its laws only when another intermingles. For example, I let a stone fall from a tower, and I judge that it will fall straight down; but it is driven to the side by the wind. I shall not, however, think that it has deviated of itself, but that a second force has intervened which has pushed it aside. So it is also with the understanding and the senses. In the case of error we appeal to the limits of reason, and thereby cast reproach not upon ourselves but upon nature. But this is wrong; for the narrow limits of reason are the causes of defect, not of error. Every error arises partly from the understanding, partly from the senses; we must therefore seek its sources in both. In so far, now, as it has sprung from the understanding, it is true. Error does sometimes arise also from the understanding; every error, therefore, as a judgment, has a partial truth. When subjective grounds are mistakenly taken for objective, this is an error: we confuse what is subjective with the constitution of the object, and through this substitution error arises. Here the conditions of the understanding are exchanged for the conditions of sensibility. A negative criterion of truth is therefore the certainty that one has not erred, that one has separated off the subjective grounds of holding-to-be-true. One has then employed the understanding alone, with the suspension of sensibility. But this seldom happens. For one cannot notice it; when one mixes in sensibility, it is like a bewitchment. For example, when someone tells a superstitious little story, he first says: I am not superstitious; and yet he finds the little story wondrous. One must, however, guard in every possible way against the influence of the understanding upon sensibility. For example, all lying is disgraceful; yet it is said that lies of necessity are permitted. But they are not. For this is mostly done from self-interest, which is an influence of sensibility upon the employment of the understanding; and here lying is just as shameful as elsewhere. In every science such an intermixture takes place, except in mathematics, where I see everything *intuitu* [by intuition]. The author speaks of a certain unavoidable error (*error invincibilis*), which [is] as certain as that a certain ignorance is unavoidable; for example, [as to] when the Last Day will come. An error is always avoidable; the best remedy is not to judge. If, however, I must judge something, for example in matters of religion, then in this case an error is sometimes unavoidable. In morals one must judge, but there no error is unavoidable; for morals carry a certain evidence with them. In judging the things of nature, for example whether there are inhabitants in the moon, there may be an unavoidable error; but here I am not obliged to judge. In all things where we do not know the absolutely great we have a mean standard: what is greater than this is admired, what is smaller is despised. So it is also with the understanding. The common understanding is the mean standard among the understandings of all men, and that which contradicts this understanding is entitled absurd. To deny common understanding to a man is an insult. Error can often be refuted by a test. – But one must seek to get at the causes and nature of the error. No error is cured by being refuted, but by being explained; for otherwise one offends the honour of other men's reason. All truths are either necessary or contingent. – The necessary must be discerned by reason *a priori*, the contingent by experience. – Both are considered by logic, although the rules of logic are necessary. Mathematics has necessary truths, and judges *a priori*. Practice and testing, however, do not confirm the proposition; they serve to elucidate these necessary truths, which are entitled apodictic truths.",[]],"busolt-s12-001":["A mark [Merkmal] signifies a partial concept of a whole representation. It does not concern a part of the thing. Wolff wrongly understands by a mark a distinguishing concept; for two things may also have a similarity. There is thus a mark of identity and a mark of diversity. Our understanding is already of itself disposed to recognise identity; to recognise diversity it must be spurred on. Marks are",[]],"busolt-s12-002":["1.) mediate, that is, a mark of a mark, a mark of a representation of a mark;",[]],"busolt-s12-003":["2.) immediate, that is, a mark of the object which does not arise through universal concepts. For example, a body is represented to me, and I say that it is a stone; then it is asked: What is a stone? It is a body which consists of very hard earth. What is earth? What is body? This is mediate. The first are entitled subordinate, the others co-ordinate; the first make up a series, the others an aggregate, and are partial representations which belong to the whole as parts (*partes*). In the series of subordinate marks there must *a priori* be a first mark. For example: What is a man? A thinking being in an organic body. What is thinking? Representing through concepts. What is representing? I can go no further; this is the first mark, and such marks are universal. The co-ordinate marks form a line without limit; but they are countable in so far as they make up a concept which they [yield] of the thing. There are, secondly, negative and affirmative marks. The former is, properly speaking, no partial concept of a thing; it is relative; the latter is a subjective mark. The negative are nothing but representations for avoiding error. There are sciences in which negative marks make up the greatest part, for example natural theology. Often one can have a negative mark of a thing, but not a positive one. There are further important and slight, fruitful and unfruitful, sufficient and insufficient marks. Sufficient marks are those which contain the whole concept of the thing. This must hold of identity.",[]],"busolt-s12-004":["Marks are either internal or external. Internal [marks are those] which concern the inner constitution and properties. The sum of all internal marks, in so far as they make up a concept, is an internal concept. All internal marks are either *notae primitiuae*, which belong to a thing alone, or *notae deriuatiuae*, where more than one mark can be posited of a thing.",[]],"busolt-s12-005":["The logical essence is the *complexus omnium conceptuum primitiuorum* [the complex of all primitive concepts]. Primitive marks are *essentialia*; the derivative marks",[]],"busolt-s12-006":["are *attributa*. The *essentialia logica* are also entitled *constitutiua*, and are regarded as grounds. The *attributa rationalia* are those which come from the grounds; [marks are] constituents [Bestandzeichen] when they belong to the essence of things not as consequences of other marks. For example, the three sides of the triangle are *essentialia*; they belong to the essence; the angles are *attributa*. In a body, for example, extension and figure [are] *constitutiua*. They belong to the essence and are *primitiua*; divisibility, mobility and mutability, on the other hand, are *deriuatiua*. *Extraessentialia* are either not internal marks at all, and are then *relationes*, or they are indeed internal, but are *modi*. That a man is rich or poor, master or servant, for example, are *relationes*. Learning is a *modus*. Clear modes of knowledge belong to logic, but obscure ones do not; they belong to psychology. That there is clear knowledge in us we can know through truth [Wahrheit], but that there is obscure knowledge in us only through inferences. Logic has nothing whatever to do with obscure representations; clear ones must be given to it. The act by which a concept becomes clear, when it was previously obscure, is entitled *euolutio conceptus*. Making a representation distinct is not *euolutio* but *analysis*, or dissection. The analysis of a concept is the concept carried successively to its partial representations. For the former logic supplies us with no means, but for the latter it does. When the marks become clear, the whole representation becomes distinct. In the concept of the triangle I have",[]],"busolt-s12-007":["1.) the concept of a surface,",[]],"busolt-s12-008":["2.) of a plane surface,",[]],"busolt-s12-009":["3.) of an enclosed surface; and in this way I have a distinct representation of a triangle.",[]],"busolt-s12-010":["In § 125 the author speaks of objective and speculative obscurity. But obscurity is always subjective. For the object which I am considering contains in itself neither distinctness nor obscurity; these arise solely through the representation or the concepts of some thing. To obscure representations we very often owe a great deal. For we should very often have been plunged by speculative reason into artificial, sophistical errors, had not certain obscure representations still held us back. Obscure representations are therefore not to be regarded as none at all, but as such as do not leave us with a sufficient concept of an object. *Completudo conceptus clari* [the completeness of a clear concept] consists in the totality of the marks which, taken together, form the concept of a thing. Concepts of this kind we seldom meet with in empirical matters. Total clearness will be found only where we are able also to connect what we think with intuition. Subjective clearness of knowledge is strength and liveliness of the understanding. All the passions can, indeed, be aroused in jest, save only disgust. Disgust is quickly aroused, but does not so soon vanish; for whereas the other passions enliven the powers of the mind, disgust depresses them. Strength is distinguished from liveliness in this, that it is a liveliness [which has] practical consequences. Liveliness is present when a thing is brought before us in intuition as if it were actually there before me. But there is also a liveliness which is not even objectively sensible. When a thing is presented in a lively manner, it must",[]],"busolt-s12-011":["1.) lead to [our] interest; this is objective;",[]],"busolt-s12-012":["2.) act upon intuition; and this is subjective.",[]],"busolt-s12-013":["To make a concept distinct does not mean to extend a concept, but to make it distinct in all its parts by means of analysis. When, however, I desire to know distinctly an object of which I do not yet have a distinct concept, this is entitled making it distinct by synthesis, that is, adding what intuition offers to knowledge. If, for instance, I know not only that gold is yellow, malleable and of a particular weight, but also that it does not rust and is fire-resistant, my concept of gold has been extended *per synthesin* [by synthesis]. Distinctness *per synthesin* can come about empirically, that is, through experience or *a posteriori*.",[]],"busolt-s12-014":["Things can be represented in various ways.",[]],"busolt-s12-015":["1.) To represent something to oneself through a concept.",[]],"busolt-s12-016":["2.) To be acquainted [kennen] [with something] through concepts.",[]],"busolt-s12-017":["3.) To know [erkennen] through the understanding, or to understand.",[]],"busolt-s12-018":["4.) To have insight [einsehen] through rules *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s12-019":["5.) To have sufficient insight into something in respect of all things.",[]],"busolt-s13-001":["Extensive magnitude rests on the distinctness of co-ordinate marks, taken as an aggregate; intensive magnitude rests on the distinctness of subordinate marks, that is, of the marks of marks. Extensive distinctness is *completudo cognitionis* [completeness of knowledge]; intensive distinctness is *profunditas cognitionis* [depth of knowledge]. *Profunditas*, or thoroughness, is the series of subordinate marks, or of the marks of marks. To perfect one's knowledge negatively does not mean to add to the aggregate of knowledge, but only to keep away whatever would hinder knowledge. Precision is present when the marks are substituted for the thing. When the concept neither has too few marks, that is, is complete, nor contains too many, that is, is precise, it is a *conceptus, qui adaequat rem* [a concept adequate to the thing].",[]],"busolt-s13-002":["Remark. Precision cannot be demanded in all sciences, but completeness [*completudinem*] can be demanded throughout. If, then, a concept is to be adequate to a thing, it must first be complete and thereafter precise. From this precision follows the elegance which mathematics possesses. For when the mathematician gives a definition, it must be so fitted to the thing that no rhetorical embellishments occur in it. Precision is thus, in the strictest sense, a logical perfection. Abundance requires permission [to determine] where it may be admitted, and this can be granted only where something is done *in favorem sensualem* [in favour of the senses].",[]],"busolt-s14-001":["Certainty is a sufficient consciousness of truth. It is therefore a state of our reason. Holding-to-be-true [Fürwahrhalten] signifies the state of the mind in so far as it judges of a thing. Certainty is objective. Certainty is:",[]],"busolt-s14-002":["1.) empirical, and",[]],"busolt-s14-003":["2.) rational, through reason, that is, apodeictically certain. The different kinds of holding-to-be-true are:",[]],"busolt-s14-004":["1.) Opining [Meinen], that is, when I hold a thing to be true although I know that I have no sufficient ground for doing so. It is neither a subjective nor an objective [sufficient] ground of holding-to-be-true. The question is raised whether we here judge assertorically or problematically. The answer is:",[]],"busolt-s14-005":["It is, properly, nothing but a problematic holding-to-be-true. Opinions are not determinate declarations. It is a sign that a man thinks when he is able to alter his opinions, when he knows how to consider them from another side. Opinion is, properly,",[]],"busolt-s14-006":["a consciousness of the insufficiency of one's own holding-to-be-true.",[]],"busolt-s14-007":["Opining is of great use. All knowledge [Kenntnisse], mathematical knowledge excepted, begins with opinions; after opinion comes the comparison of marks, and then certainty.",[]],"busolt-s14-008":["2.) Believing [Glauben]: this is a subjectively sufficient ground of holding-to-be-true, together with the consciousness that it is objectively insufficient. Opining is neither a subjectively nor an objectively sufficient ground of holding-to-be-true; believing is only a subjectively sufficient ground. This believing carries no objective necessity, because another may have equally weighty grounds for not holding the thing to be true. At the outset we must judge even upon insufficient grounds; for they are a part of certainty.",[]],"busolt-s14-009":["3.) Knowing [Wissen]. This is a holding-to-be-true which is subjectively sufficient, together with the certainty that it is also objectively sufficient. Believing we restrict to the testimony of another; but on this we may also already ground knowing. Where there are no documents, we only believe. Yet even when I have quite distinct marks, I still cannot say that I know it with certainty; for something may still be insufficient. Best of all is the language of modesty, namely, to say: I believe it. In believing, a practical interest always enters along with speculative knowledge, and this interest is of such a kind that no one can argue it away. The ground of holding-to-be-true through belief is often more sufficient than [that of] knowing. Remark. In opining I judge problematically, in believing assertorically, in knowing apodeictically. The objectively sufficient holding-to-be-true, even though it [counts] as logically insufficient, is sufficient for belief in knowledge, without regard to the act of logical holding-to-be-true. Here the reference is to historical belief. There is also a rational belief [Vernunftglaube]. When practical holding-to-be-true is necessary in a certain respect, it is a rational belief. It is remarkable that a rational belief can occur; but through reason a man can indeed believe that there is a God. In speculative or philosophical knowledge one may opine, but only about empirical things. When, however, there are things that concern reason alone, I cannot opine; I must have grounds either for believing or for knowing. In empirical matters we can form opinions, because, if we follow them up, we proceed along the guiding thread of insufficient grounds, and may well gradually arrive at certainty and be able to confirm [the opinion]. For instance, it was at first believed that thunderstorms might have a similarity to electricity, and this was afterwards confirmed by experience.",[]],"busolt-s14-010":["In mathematics we have not opinions but certainty. In philosophy too opinions may occur, [namely] in order to refute the opinions of others. Believing applies, properly, only to practical modes of knowledge. It is sometimes used also of empirical ones, as, for instance, [the question] whether the moon has inhabitants. Certainty is either empirical or rational. Empirically I can be certain of a thing that it exists, not that it is necessary. The latter requires a rational certainty; where reason settles something, experience counts for little. As, for instance, if someone, after demonstrating the proposition that all the angles in a triangle amount to 180 degrees, wished to take a protractor and measure them. Rational certainty is either apodeictic or moral. A speculative certainty is apodeictic, a practical one moral. The apodeictic is the relation of knowledge to the understanding; the moral is the relation to the will. Moral certainty is that which I can assert with complete conscientiousness. Apodeictic logical certainty is either (a) intuitive, that is, mathematical certainty, or (b) discursive, that is, philosophical certainty.",[]],"busolt-s14-011":["This [latter] can have apodeictic certainty, but not intuition. Apodeictic certainty is a complete certainty *a priori*. Mathematical and philosophical certainty agree in this, that they are apodeictic certainties; the difference lies in this, that the one is intuitive and the other discursive. Mathematical certainty carries with it a certain evidence *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s14-012":["When knowledge is not objectively sufficient, it is persuasion [Überredung]. Many a man believes himself convinced when he is merely persuaded. He who lets himself be easily persuaded shows a weakness of understanding. We employ the expressions 'accept' and 'reject'.",[]],"busolt-s14-013":["They seem to refer to the will, and do in fact refer to it. Accepting applies only where there is no apodeictic certainty. If I am certain of a thing, if the grounds are sufficient, I must accept it. To accept something I need not be certain, and in that case I have accepted it with a limitation. In philosophy such acceptances are very good, and are a postponement, a suspension, of judgment. This suspension is either critical or sceptical. A critical suspension is when I postpone my judgments in order to pass them later. In such a suspension I say: *non liquet* [it is not clear]. A sceptical suspension is when I leave the matter always in obscurity.",[]],"busolt-s14-014":["Of such a suspension it is better to say that one gives up one's judgment, renounces it, than that one suspends it.",[]],"busolt-s14-015":["The dogmatists settle the matter forthwith, and do not suspend their judgment. Many a man is dogmatic by nature, commonly the proud, who have great self-conceit. But they are also at times struck down again by the sceptics, who likewise have their self-conceit, in that they always renounce judgment. To postpone one's judgment is useful in philosophy, and it is well to accustom oneself to it early. One must not straightway close one's mind. But where it is a matter of the practical, one must be resolute. We have two kinds of judgment:",[]],"busolt-s14-016":["a. a provisional,",[]],"busolt-s14-017":["b. a determinate.",[]],"busolt-s14-018":["We judge provisionally at all times, however few grounds we may have for the one side or the other. For instance, when a man enters a company, everyone at once passes a judgment on his character from his features. When a man looks pale, we are apt at once to infer that he is sickly. But it is a fault to take a provisional judgment for a determinate one, for appearances may deceive. Provisional judgments lead us to determinate ones. Provisional judgments may indeed already be correct; and when, from impatience, we do not apply them towards [reaching] determinate judgments, but take them themselves for such, a principle arises, and this is entitled prejudice [Vorurteil]. From such reasonable provisional judgments arise proverbs and the canons of the schools; these must not be taken for determinate judgments.",[]],"busolt-s14-019":["We have subjective grounds of judgment (they might better be entitled causes of judgment), [and prejudice arises] when we wrongly take these subjective grounds for objective ones, so that a *vitium subreptionis* [fallacy of subreption] arises. To this there may contribute:",[]],"busolt-s14-020":["a.) Opinion, [that is,] predilection, namely, that everything [connected with it] seems to us worthy of approval and, even if it is bad, nevertheless deserving of excuse.",[]],"busolt-s14-021":["There is also a predilection which is an *esprit du corps*, whereby one holds everything [of one's body] to be true; further, there is a predilection for one's fatherland, and a predilection for what is one's own.",[]],"busolt-s14-022":["b.) Habit. This is very bad, since even the most distinct proofs convince those who are subject to it only in the course of a long time. This is found in all men, especially in those who do not willingly depart from their concepts. One must refrain from habituating oneself to anything, that is, from making it a necessity.",[]],"busolt-s14-023":["It is customary also to give the name of prejudice to what flows from prejudice; and so a genuine prejudice is a principle. A prejudice is a principle [taken] from the understanding or reason in a positive sense. Understanding or reason consists in activity; it is active, it has spontaneity; the senses are passive. In prejudice reason is merely passive, for prejudice arises from inclination, and inclination is in itself passive. We can therefore have reason also in a passive state. We have two acts of the spontaneity of the understanding:",[]],"busolt-s14-024":["a.) Reflection, or deliberation [Überlegung], when we compare our judgment with the laws of the understanding.",[]],"busolt-s14-025":["b.) Investigation, when I seek straightway to get at the ground of judgments. Certain judgments require investigation, but many do not. Reflection, however, must take place in every judgment, [namely as to] whether it holds subjectively or objectively.",[]],"busolt-s14-026":["Sometimes we judge without reflection; that is, we do not reflect whether the judgment springs from the understanding, i.e. is objective, or whether the senses have intruded, i.e. [whether it is] subjective, inclination having slipped something in. Hence it can easily come about that men take subjective causes for objective ones; and then prejudice arises, [which] is a propensity to take subjective causes for objective ones. A prejudice is found only where there is a propensity; it is *propensio ad iudicia ex causis subiectivis* [a propensity to judgments from subjective causes]. Such a prejudice arises from imitation, inclination, habit. Imitation occurs not only in children but also in older people. To this belong the proverbs of the populace, the sentences of learned men and poets, the canons of the schools.",[]],"busolt-s14-027":["Precipitancy is a lack of reflection on the part of the understanding. Prejudice comes about through a propensity, not through precipitancy. The propensity which has arisen from recent habit makes prejudices a necessity. There are:",[]],"busolt-s14-028":["a. Prejudices of authority. This is grounded on the esteem which we have for others. Authority is accorded either to the person, [or rests on] the esteem which we have for the multitude, and for what all the world says is good. Further, we also ground correctness on this. The judgment of the multitude is a criterion of truth. For since the multitude comprises different subjects, while the judgment of the multitude must be subjective, that is, they must all agree in one, we accept the judgment as correct.",[]],"busolt-s14-029":["b.) The prejudice of antiquity. From the end of the last century until now the prejudice in favour of novelties prevailed.",[]],"busolt-s14-030":["Now, however, people wish once more to extol the ancient writings. Fontenelle published a work on ancient and modern writers, in which he says that we ought not to give the ancients any preference, for we are all made of one stuff. He says that we have moreover this advantage over them, that we have their writings as preliminary exercises, which they did not have. We have a prejudice in favour of antiquity:",[]],"busolt-s14-031":["a.) Because we believe that the sciences decline with the years, [and that] nature grows older and deteriorates.",[]],"busolt-s14-032":["b.) We take the knowing of the ancients for erudition, and thereby esteem antiquity itself highly. In historical matters, however, we do owe the ancients a great deal.",[]],"busolt-s14-033":["c.) We take our astonishment [Verwunderung] for admiration [Bewunderung]. Astonishment concerns sensibility, inasmuch as we did not expect something. Admiration concerns the object itself, namely what is excellent [in it]. So it is with antiquity: we are astonished that the ancients already thought in this way. In the case of a learned woman we are astonished at her learning, because it is something rare, but we do not admire it. This astonishment comes very close to the impression of admiration.",[]],"busolt-s14-034":["d.) Our esteem for antiquity comes from this, that since the books of the ancients which remain to us, these few products, escaped the destructive power [of time], we believe that what has preserved itself through so long a time must assuredly be the best.",[]],"busolt-s14-035":["Remark. The favourable prejudice in favour of antiquity prevails especially among men of letters [Literatoren], as those who are properly occupied with reading the ancient writers. These now believe that they already find new discoveries in the writings of the ancients. But commonly they do not find them there until they have already been discovered. Thus Aristarchus is said to have already had the Copernican system; and in like manner people seek to prove, from the word *versorum*, that the ancients had knowledge of magnetic force.",[]],"busolt-s14-036":["e.) Gratitude towards the ancients is likewise one ground of the favourable prejudice in their favour; and in truth they do always remain the heroes of learning and the first pioneers. Thus in philosophy Aristotle, in mathematics Euclid and Archimedes, are always the fathers; so too in models of taste, and of the popularity which prevails in them, they will always remain our masters. We owe them, then, a great deal, and may therefore indeed judge them, but not condemn them. Yet, especially in our modern times, this obligation is carried too far, in that, in the learning of the sciences, the way of reading [*lecturae*] is recommended more than that of reflection. Wide reading in the ancient writings is certainly to be recommended, but one must not therewith abandon the way of reflection.",[]],"busolt-s14-037":["1.) The envy of the learned towards one another. For just as the learned owe their power solely to the jealousy [Missgunst] of the books, in that, as it were from long weariness of the long pomp, they transfer dominion to a single one, who makes them all equal, so it is in respect of the ancients.",[]],"busolt-s14-038":["2.) The prejudice of novelty. Under this we may believe that we shall advance further than the ancients.",[]],"busolt-s14-039":["3.) The prejudice of an accepted doctrinal system. Those sciences which are grounded on a dogmatic doctrinal system are not free from such prejudices, nor is philosophy. Such a prejudice is present when we refuse to accept, even on the best objective grounds, anything that is contrary to our system.",[]],"busolt-s14-040":["4.) Egoism. This is",[]],"busolt-s14-041":["a.) moral, that is, when one holds oneself alone to be of worth, but not others;",[]],"busolt-s14-042":["b.) logical, when one holds one's own judgment to be decisive, and all other judgments, on the contrary, to be superfluous and their contradiction to be a matter of indifference. One pays no heed whatsoever to agreeing judgments.",[]],"busolt-s14-043":["5.) The prejudice of sensibility, that is, a kind of laziness. For one gives common understanding preference over all the sciences, and believes that everything can be settled by common understanding. One does not duly distinguish the popular use of the understanding from the scientific. A science also falls under suspicion when it wholly contradicts common understanding. The prejudices here cited were prejudices of trust.",[]],"busolt-s14-044":["The prejudice of mistrust is present when one is prepossessed against antiquity or against novelty. Besides these there is also found a certain melancholy way of thinking, which commonly tends to set in gradually with increasing experience. It is twofold:",[]],"busolt-s14-045":["a.) In the practical, that is, in respect of the morals of men and of their actions. Of this the historians, for instance, can give us the very best ideas. When in all ages we become aware of so assiduous an endeavour of mortals to harm one another, we at last become mistrustful of all men, and banish fidelity and honesty from the whole civilised world. This is entitled misanthropy.",[]],"busolt-s14-046":["b.) In the theoretical, in respect of philosophy; this is entitled misology, or a hatred of reason. [It arises] when learned men, after having taken so much trouble, at last find themselves unsatisfied in the most important and most necessary questions, to which their reason is not equal; for instance: Whence am I? What shall I be after death? A Condamine, a Rousseau can provide examples of this. This prejudice arises mostly from one's having at first promised oneself far too much. There is further a prejudice against [other] nations. It is a common prejudice, and is found especially among the French. The German nation, on the other hand, does not have it; it has rather a propensity to imitate others. There is also a prejudice of mistrust against oneself. This is something rare, since one more often trusts oneself more rather than less. Nevertheless it does occur, and indeed in persons who could have trusted themselves more. Probability: *Probabilitas est dimidium certitudinis; possibilis modus id est, quod gradum quendam probabilitatis habet* [probability is half of certainty; the possible mode is that which has a certain degree of probability]. It refers to objective grounds, *verisimilitudo* [verisimilitude], however, to subjective ones. Although in all these the grounds are insufficient, they are nevertheless always grounds of holding-to-be-true. All probabilities are a relation of truth to certainty; consequently the ground must here be compared with sufficient grounds. There can be no objective criterion of probability. The honesty and sincerity",[]],"busolt-s14-047":["of witnesses seems indeed to be an objective criterion of truth, but it is not. It only draws our attention to the criteria of probability; therefore they give us no rules at all by which we can judge what is probable. The judging of probability is not a work of the understanding but of the faculty of judgment, which shows us whether [something] stands under the rules of the understanding or not. All observations that are made thus differ among themselves both *in excessu* and *in defectu* [by excess and by defect]. The mean of all these observations can be taken as the most probable. To specify the rules of probability without distinction is wholly impossible.",[]],"busolt-s14-048":["Doubt is a subjectively insufficient ground which is opposed to holding-to-be-true. It is the means by which truth is weakened, but at the same time also an *organon* for its confirmation. When doubts are refuted, when they are removed, doubts arise once more from their removal, [namely] whether it is really a step towards nearer insight into the truth or not. Thereby I now confirm myself in the grounds of refutation which flow from the truth that was doubted; consequently I shall also bring this [truth] nearer. Both in holding-to-be-true and in objections I begin with obscure representations. Before we raise objections, holding-to-be-true begins to become doubtful; one is unwilling to concede this or that, yet cannot make it known to the other; and so a scruple arises. A scruple is therefore a ground of doubt in so far as it is not clearly known; it occurs especially in those who cannot bring their objections to distinctness. Scruples are found [arising] from subjective causes. These cannot be refuted, for they concern the state of mind, [and] habit. In the case of an objection we have a twofold course:",[]],"busolt-s14-049":["a.) when one dismisses it,",[]],"busolt-s14-050":["b.) when one answers it. A proposition which has not been doubted is entitled an established truth. This remains correct even after long doubts, and retains a certainty. In human reason we can have subjective grounds for asserting a thing objectively. These latter are principles. Scepticism denies to reason all certainty, and holds it to be uncertain. The sceptic says: there are no objective assertions. But precisely in saying it, he contradicts himself. 'Nothing can be determined as certain' is their universal principle. Yet they do determine something when they say that everyone has only subjective assertions. A certain resolve is entitled a maxim; this is a subjective practical principle of reason, that is, a resolution in respect of the object to make use of one's reason in a certain way; for instance: I will never lie, even if I could gain a great deal thereby. Every method requires certain subjective rules according to which modes of knowledge are combined; and a man without maxims has no reliability at all. The maxims of reflection are:",[]],"busolt-s14-051":["a.) The maxims of the dogmatic use, that is, the resolve so to use one's reason that one hopes to attain apodeictic certainty. *Maxima confidentia in certitudinem cognitionis humanae est dogmatica* [the maxim of confidence in the certainty of human knowledge is dogmatic].",[]],"busolt-s14-052":["b.) The maxims of the sceptical use, in which one always doubts of attaining certainty. *Maxima diffidentia in certitudinem cognitionis humanae est sceptica* [the maxim of distrust in the certainty of human knowledge is sceptical]. There have been learned men of both kinds. The sceptics called themselves also *Zetetici* [seekers]. Of the two kinds, each proved the groundlessness of the other. The school of Plato was called the Academy; of this there were three schools, of which the second fell into scepticism. Arcesilaus was its founder. There is also another school of sceptics, who called themselves Pyrrhonists. They differed from the Academics in this, that the latter maintained that there is no truth at all, whereas the former only placed a doubt therein; they adopted only a postponement. There is an unlimited trust, and also an unlimited mistrust, in human reason. He is *kat' exochen* [pre-eminently] dogmatic who has an unlimited trust in human reason [proceeding] from concepts; the opposite is *kat' exochen* sceptical. The sceptical maxim entirely abolishes all utility of the use of reason, etc. The dogmatic maxim seems at first to be of great utility, but afterwards one finds otherwise. The critic stands between the two. This critical use of reason is to be recommended among the kinds of use of reason cited, because it judges only when the sources have been sufficiently examined. Criticism is always something permissible, indeed even necessary, because only thereby can human knowledge attain sufficient certainty. It is only a pity that this freedom of criticism is so greatly misused. Criticism is not employed until the understanding has attained a certain maturity.",[]],"busolt-s15-001":["A hypothesis is the holding-to-be-true of a mode of knowledge in which I infer from its sufficiency to its true consequences; for instance, when I know that there is fire beneath the earth. The knowledge, therefore, from which many true consequences flow is a hypothesis. When a ground has nothing but true consequences, it is a true ground; but I do not yet know this *a priori*, but *a posteriori*, or through a hypothesis. A hypothesis is a presupposition. A ground which has nothing but true consequences ceases to be [merely] a true opinion; it becomes certain. A hypothesis can never be brought to apodeictic certainty. For instance, the system of Copernicus is only a hypothesis. From given consequences we cannot infer with safety to determinate grounds, unless it can also be shown that the consequences flow from a single ground.",[]],"busolt-s15-002":["To the perfection of a hypothesis there belongs the following:",[]],"busolt-s15-003":["a.) The possibility of the hypothesis must be certain. No hypothesis is apodeictically certain. It follows from this that in metaphysics there are no hypotheses; in physics, on the other hand, they are necessary.",[]],"busolt-s15-004":["b.) The consequence of the consequences derived from this assumed ground must be certain.",[]],"busolt-s15-005":["c.) The sufficiency of the assumed ground for all possible consequences remains uncertain.",[]],"busolt-s15-006":["d.) There belongs to it the unity of the assumed ground, which constitutes the whole probability of the hypothesis. The more subsidiary hypotheses are needed, the less probable is the hypothesis. To infer from consequences to grounds involves an uncertainty, because one and the same mode of knowledge can have more than one ground, and because a consequence flows from more than one ground. The correctness of such a ground is proved through the consequences, but it can never be brought to apodeictic certainty.",[]],"busolt-s16-001":["Complete holding-to-be-true together with consciousness is entitled conviction [Überzeugung]. Holding-to-be-true from subjective grounds which are sufficient in a practical relation is belief. Holding-to-be-true of which one does not know whether it arises from subjective or from objective grounds is persuasion. An orator's chief aim is to persuade. Assent is to arise, even though he knows of neither objective nor subjective sufficiency.",[]],"busolt-s16-002":["Knowing and being able [Können] are distinguished thus: when we know that something ought to happen, but cannot carry it out, the former is called knowing and the latter being able. Properly, not every knowing makes [bedingt] a science, for a science constitutes a system. Nor is every being able an art, for art is the capacity to produce something according to ideas. In every science which constitutes a system there is also art; for",[]],"busolt-s16-003":["1.) in every system there is an,",[]],"busolt-s16-004":["2.) it is always an art to bring something into systematic order.",[]],"busolt-s16-005":["The idea is the ground of the manifold in respect of the product of [men's] heads. The artist acts according to a system, the craftsman through imitation. The artist and the learned man also have a resemblance. Rhapsodies of modes of knowledge can be very rich and yet be no system. [A system] rests on the systematic unity of the idea of the whole. Sciences require a convincing knowledge of the totality of the whole, and this must be preceded by the idea of the whole, which determines in advance the place of each part; as, for instance, at the beginning of the logic we spoke of matter and form, of canon and *organon*, etc. It is the same with art: it comes from *können* [to be able], and is distinguished in this from craft. An exercise which requires skill through imitation is craft; one through ideas is art. For instance, is an architect an artist? If he works according to a plan, he is only a copyist; but he exercises [his skill] according to another's idea, which he must be able to grasp. Such a man is entitled a virtuoso; but he who has originality of ideas, that is, his own genius, is an artist, and when he builds a house in this way he is an architect [Baukünstler]. One also acts according to ideas which cannot be taught; they cannot be set forth, and even if this were possible, another could not at the same time act according to these rules. These too are arts, and not sciences; for instance, the art of reckoning, the art of poetry, etc. There are also arts which sciences precede; for instance, glass-grinding according to optics. What are sciences? The sum of knowledge is either an aggregate or a system, whether these modes of knowledge be historical or rational. Both aggregate and system are a whole. A system is that whole whose combination rests on an idea. In an aggregate the parts precede the whole; in a system the idea of the whole precedes the putting together of the parts. Every sum of modes of knowledge is entitled a science in so far as these modes of knowledge constitute a system. All historical knowledge, as well as all self-teaching [Autodidaxie], is always an aggregate and not a system.",[]],"busolt-s17-001":["There is an apodeictic and an empirical proof. One can sometimes prove something empirically, yet not give it apodeictic certainty; for instance, whether the sea once stood over the dry land. To the empirical proof is opposed the proof of reason *a priori*. A proof which cannot attain apodeictic certainty is an analogical proof. Both the apodeictic and the analogical proof are either discursive or intuitive. The latter is proof through the construction of concepts, the former through concepts. An intuitive apodeictic proof can occur only in mathematics; for:",[]],"busolt-s17-002":["1.) it is to be an apodeictic proof *a priori*,",[]],"busolt-s17-003":["2.) [it is to] be known through intuition *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s17-004":["For this there are required not only concepts but also the construction of concepts, through which one becomes capable of an intuitive concept; and this is demonstration. It can therefore occur only in mathematics. This apodeictic certainty, whether it be discursive or intuitive, is entitled certainty of the first rank; empirical certainty constitutes that of the second rank. The author speaks also of arbitrary truth. If something is arbitrarily true, I can take it as I please. When something is assumed which is not objectively determined, this is the arbitrary truth of a proposition, and is entitled *positio*; for instance, that we count from 1 to 10, that there are 360 degrees in a circle, etc. Now from proofs there is required:",[]],"busolt-s17-005":["a.) the knowledge proved;",[]],"busolt-s17-006":["b.) the connection of the grounds of proof with the knowledge;",[]],"busolt-s17-007":["c.) a certainty which consists in this connection.",[]],"busolt-s17-008":["No immediately certain proposition is, therefore, a proof. The form of the grounds rests on distinctness. This may be intuitive or discursive. The distinctness of the connection of a mode of knowledge",[]],"busolt-s17-009":["[the connection of a proposition] or judgment with its grounds gives the demonstration its discursive distinctness. If one considers a proof in general [*generaliter*], one can take the *probatio* from various grounds. I can ground it empirically; but this yields no apodeictic certainty. Every proof of reason must be complete. Here, too, a distinction is drawn between a proof [Beweis] and a ground of proof [Beweisgrund]. Every ground of proof is an incomplete proof; only complete certainty, the distinctness of the connection with the grounds of proof, constitutes the proof. An empirical proof shows the truth of the proposition from actuality, as it is met with in other things. An apodeictic proof, or proof of reason, shows the necessity [of the proposition] from the constitution of the thing itself. A proof is a distinct knowledge of the correct connection of a proposition with sufficient grounds. A proof can therefore be false",[]],"busolt-s17-010":["(a) *in materia* [in its matter];",[]],"busolt-s17-011":["(b) *in consequentia* [in its consequence], which happens for the most part in proofs by analogy.",[]],"busolt-s17-012":["This consequence is the connection of the grounds with the consequences. An apodeictic proof is therefore either a philosophical *probatio* or a mathematical *demonstratio*. A proof by analogy sometimes seems to be apodeictic, because in a discursive mode of knowledge many concepts often lie concealed. The author speaks of a demonstrable and an indemonstrable mode of knowledge. The word 'indemonstrable' is, however, equivocal; for it also means 'groundless'. [Truths that are indemonstrable in the proper sense] are principles [Grundsätze], and are the immediate certainty of a thing. They are the ultimate ground of all mediate [certainties], and require no proof. Immediate, independent certainties must necessarily contain in themselves the ground of the dependent ones. Proofs are further divided into",[]],"busolt-s17-013":["(a) direct and",[]],"busolt-s17-014":["(b) indirect proofs. The former are entitled ostensive, the latter apagogic proofs.",[]],"busolt-s17-015":["The former is when one proves the truth of the proposition directly, through weighty grounds; the latter, when one proves the falsity of its opposite. This [latter] rests on the principle that every proposition whose opposite is false is true.",[]],"busolt-s17-016":["Apagogic proofs belong not so much to mathematics as to philosophy. A proof which has complete certainty",[]],"busolt-s17-017":["is also entitled a demonstration. A demonstration ought properly to be an apodeictic certainty, a certainty through reason, and indeed an intuitive one, *cum intuitu a priori* [with *a priori* intuition]; for were it *a posteriori*, it would be empirical. All demonstration is therefore mathematical. The mania for demonstration [Demonstrirsucht] is the affectation of introducing demonstrations into philosophy. It is also entitled a mania for demonstration when one proves things that stand in no need of proof. This is found chiefly in disputations. Logic presupposes a concept of experience. We prove *a posteriori* when we prove through experience, *a priori* when we prove through reason. Belief [Glaube] is not a source of knowledge. I can believe in general [*generaliter*], that is, I believe a mode of knowledge and not a person; this takes place through reason. Belief is the necessary presupposition of a mode of knowledge in connection with a practical interest. We cannot believe where no practical interest is involved. To believe something is therefore possible",[]],"busolt-s17-018":["(a) through mere reason, but also",[]],"busolt-s17-019":["(b) through testimony.",[]],"busolt-s17-020":["Here I believe someone, and this is historical belief. Rational belief [Vernunftglaube] is to be distinguished from reasonable belief [vernünftiger Glaube]; for historical belief must also be reasonable. A reasonable belief is one that has sufficient grounds for accepting something.",[]],"busolt-s17-021":["Can I believe historically the *objecta* of rational belief? If I already believe through reason, it would be absurd to believe historically. But if our reason does not suffice, we can believe others; this, however, only in mathematics, not in philosophy. On the testimony of others I can accept something only historically, and the object of this is experience. The pronouncements of God, however, we can believe; for the concept of God is bound up with the concept of the most perfect understanding. Yet this belief still comes down to belief in men; for we believe those who wrote down the Bible. No one, therefore, can report anything other than his own experience; and yet there is a way of acquiring modes of knowledge in which we believe something on the testimony of others. This rests:",[]],"busolt-s17-022":["(a) on the correctness of the testimony;",[]],"busolt-s17-023":["(b) on the sincerity of the witnesses, [namely, that they] say nothing more than what actually happened.",[]],"busolt-s17-024":["The Greeks and Romans paid no heed to this. Curtius, for example, is full of falsehoods in the speeches which he put into people's mouths. This principle has, indeed, been established among scholars only for the last hundred and fifty years. The object of historical belief is therefore experience, in that I increase my own through the experience of others. We can have experience from others; we can also have experiences of our own. In the case of the experiences of others, we must look to the competence and the standing of the witnesses. Unbelief we can divide into rational unbelief and historical unbelief. One who is rationally an unbeliever might also be entitled morally an unbeliever; for reason in the end issues in morality. Such unbelief is greatly to be censured, for it overthrows all morality. Historical unbelief, on the other hand, is not; for it involves nothing more than a lack of insight into the credibility of the witnesses. Scholars have more reason to believe historically than the common man; for in this they rely on the reputation of [other] scholars. Historical unbelief therefore shows either",[]],"busolt-s17-025":["(a) ignorance, or",[]],"busolt-s17-026":["(b) an evil heart, in that one declares all men liars and maliciously refuses to believe their writings. Here one rejects all sincerity and all trustworthiness among men; and in a certain respect this too is moral unbelief.",[]],"busolt-s18-001":["As we have theoretical, so also we have practical knowledge. The former represents how something is, the latter how something ought to be. In like manner, speculative and pragmatic knowledge are distinguished. The latter must contain the motives [Triebfedern] to action. Even pragmatic modes of knowledge, and indeed every representation, if it does not at the same time contain the impulse to action, have their ground in speculation. Practical knowledge is not, however, always pragmatic; for it is not an impulse [telling me] that I ought to do something, but only precepts given for the case in which one should wish to act upon them. What the author goes on to say in this chapter does not belong to logic. A theoretical mode of knowledge can, again, be pragmatic: for instance, whether God is just, omnipresent, etc. If we know this, we seek to act rightly.",[]],"busolt-s19-001":["Logic has to do solely with the understanding. The *operationes mentis* [operations of the mind] were rightly divided even by the ancients, namely into *apprehensio simplex*, that is, *conceptus*, *iudicium* and *ratiocinium* [simple apprehension or concept, judgment, and inference]. Every representation we obtain through intuition, *per intuitum*; but *intuitus* is properly *repraesentatio singularis* [a singular representation], while *conceptus est repraesentatio generalis* [a concept is a general representation]. The *intuitus* in mathematics is universal:",[]],"busolt-s19-002":["for this *intuitus* takes place *per constructionem conceptuum* [through the construction of concepts]. *Repraesentatio cuius mihi conscius sum, est perceptio* [a representation of which I am conscious is perception].",[]],"busolt-s19-003":["*Repraesentatio obiective spectata est cognitio* [a representation regarded objectively is knowledge].",[]],"busolt-s19-004":["*Repraesentatio subiective spectata est sensatio* [a representation regarded subjectively is sensation]. Knowledge is the agreement of a representation with the object. All knowledge is either *intuitus* or *conceptus*. The *conceptus* is, moreover, divided:",[]],"busolt-s19-005":["(a) into the *conceptus sensitivus* or *empiricus*, where we arrive at something through intuition;",[]],"busolt-s19-006":["(b) into the *conceptus intellectualis purus*, when the representation rests solely on the understanding; and this is a *notio*.",[]],"busolt-s19-007":["The idea [is the concept] of the *conceptus rationis*, for which no object whatsoever can be given in experience; the use of the *notio*, on the other hand, can lie in experience, and finds its objects here in experience. — *Perceptio obiective spectata* is therefore *cognitio*. This is either sensible or intellectual. Sensible modes of knowledge *a posteriori* are entitled empirical. *Conceptus sensitivi* are those in which the *objectum* is given through the senses. The senses do not, properly speaking, give a *conceptus*, [just as] the understanding is not *intuitus*; but the senses give me the matter for the *conceptus*, whereas the *conceptus* themselves are a work of the understanding. For the understanding is the faculty of knowing through concepts. A *conceptus* can accordingly be *formaliter* a product of the understanding and *materialiter* a product of the senses.",[]],"busolt-s19-008":["Note. With respect to the senses, the word properly used is not 'product' but 'result', because the representations of the senses are not *repraesentationes sentenciae* [?], but are given.",[]],"busolt-s19-009":["A *conceptus* can also be merely *intellectualis*. From this very fact arises the logical form. Such a *conceptus* is entitled *notio*, *et oppositum huius est conceptus sensitivus, seu potius empiricus* [and its opposite is the sensitive, or rather the empirical, concept]. These *notiones* can be exhibited *in concreto*. But concepts *a priori* of which no object can be given *in concreto* are ideas; they go beyond experience, and yet are not chimeras.",[]],"busolt-s19-010":["The *conceptus* were divided into *conceptus dati* and *factitii seu arbitrarii* [given, and made or arbitrary, concepts]. *Conceptus dati* are the concepts which are given to me of a thing; *conceptus factitii*, those which I have actually made for myself. For example, every inventor of a work of art has a *conceptum arbitrarium seu factitium* of the works to be produced. *Conceptus dati sunt dati vel per experientiam*, as the *conceptus coloris rubri*, *vel per naturam intellectus* [given concepts are given either through experience, as the concept of the colour red, or through the nature of the understanding], as the concept of substance. Representations are either *a priori* or empirical. We have",[]],"busolt-s19-011":["(a) *conceptus factitios a priori*;",[]],"busolt-s19-012":["(b) *conceptus factitios a posteriori*. Mathematics is concerned with *conceptibus factitiis*. The *conceptus dati* are either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The latter are empirical, the former pure. For example, all concepts of colours and of taste are *conceptus dati a posteriori*; the concept of virtue is [a *conceptus datus*] *a priori*.",[]],"busolt-s19-013":["The question for logic is how representations become concepts. [Logicians] therefore consider the form. How does a concept arise out of intuition? For this there is required",[]],"busolt-s19-014":["(a) reflection, or attention to the manifold that is thought in the intuition;",[]],"busolt-s19-015":["(b) comparison, or the comparing of difference and identity: for example, the comparison of spruces and firs with willows; the former have needles, the latter leaves; I thus have a concept of needle-bearing and of leaf-bearing trees. Both these steps belong to the positive [side]; to the negative belongs",[]],"busolt-s19-016":["(c) *abstractio*, in which I disregard all differences and consider only the identity.",[]],"busolt-s19-017":["A concept is that which is contained in many things, the *repraesentatio communis* [common representation]. *Repraesentatio communis est quae efficit identitatem* [a common representation is that which effects the identity] of the manifold. In the *intuitus* I can have more representations; in the *conceptus* only those which are common to different things. *Usus conceptuum est vel in abstracto, vel in concreto, non vero conceptus ipse* [it is the use of concepts that is either *in abstracto* or *in concreto*, not the concept itself]. [The use is] *in abstracto* when I separate a concept from all manifoldness, for example, the concept of man in general.",[]],"busolt-s19-018":["These are of great utility, and must precede the others. For how, for example, can I form a concept of a king unless I first form a concept of man? Abstract concepts are reproached with being unintelligible and therefore unfruitful; to this one replies that from them one takes only a mark common to many things, by means of which one can set out a great deal.",[]],"busolt-s19-019":["From the concept *in abstracto* I must gradually descend to the concept *in concreto*, since otherwise there is no application. Concepts are divided into higher and lower. The matter is what I represent to myself in the concept. A concept can therefore contain more or fewer representations. The extension [Umfang] consists in what stands under the concept, and in what belongs to all the manifold [comprised under it]. This involves a certain magnitude of the concept, that is, of its extension. One concept can therefore have a greater sphere, a wider application, than another. The more I think a concept *in abstracto*, the greater its sphere becomes, and the content diminishes. The more I think a concept *in concreto*, the smaller its sphere becomes; but it grows in content. A *conceptus singularis* has no sphere at all. A concept is entitled *superior* only in consideration and comparison of those standing under it; the one standing under it is entitled *inferior*. The distinction is therefore relative. A *conceptus* must have a sphere. The *conceptus* which comprehends another under itself is entitled *genus*; that which is contained in another is entitled *species*. For example, iron is a species of metal. A genus, considered in relation to another, can also be a species: metal, for example, is a species of body. Further, a species can also be a genus: iron, for example, is the genus of bar-iron. The distinction consists in the relation; and so we always proceed from genus to species, until at last we arrive at the *individuum*. The greater the concept *in abstracto*, the greater the sphere. The *conceptus summus*, which has the greatest sphere, has the highest genus; and from this first genus we descend by degrees [*gradatim*] to the *individuum*, which might be entitled *conceptus singularis*. We can compare [concepts] by their sphere only when they are of the same kind. The difference between the species which belong under the same genus is the *differentia specifica*. *Differentia numerica est, si res specie eadem sunt numero diverso* [numerical difference is when things the same in species are numerically different]. The *conceptus superior* is entitled *latior* [wider]; the *inferior*, *angustior* [narrower]. But when the spheres of the *conceptus* are equal, that is, when the difference between them is infinitely small, they are *conceptus reciproci*. The author speaks of the *conceptus universalis et particularis*; but it is only the use of concepts that is either universal or particular. Perhaps by *conceptus universalis* he understands the *conceptus communis*; but this every concept is.",[]],"busolt-s20-001":["Definitions are distinctly determined concepts. They are:",[]],"busolt-s20-002":["(a) analytic and",[]],"busolt-s20-003":["(b) synthetic.",[]],"busolt-s20-004":["The analytic is the definition of a given concept, in which the manifold is made distinct through analysis [Zergliederung]. The synthetic is the definition of a made concept, that is, the arbitrary combination of the manifold, whereby I make distinct a concept of the thing. To analysis there belongs [the following]:",[]],"busolt-s20-005":["(1) A concept must be given.",[]],"busolt-s20-006":["(2) I must add nothing to it, but must make it distinct to myself through analysis. In synthesis I can say: I will, for example, that a tetrahedron be a body composed of four equilateral faces.",[]],"busolt-s20-007":["Synthesis can be *a priori* and also *a posteriori*. In respect of all made concepts a definition must be given. Mathematics always begins with definitions, for it has to do solely with made concepts; it is a pure synthesis of magnitude. It does indeed also treat of space; but since this is not a made concept, it does not define it, but takes it as known to everyone. A synthetic definition of a thing cannot be false; for through it I make the *definitum*, that is, the concept which I have of the thing through the definition.",[]],"busolt-s20-008":["A given concept is given either *a priori*, for example, the concept of virtue, of space, etc., or *a posteriori*, for example, the concept of metal. Both are defined through analysis. *A priori*, for example: if I am to define cause, I analyse the concept of cause and find that there belongs to it",[]],"busolt-s20-009":["(a) a thing that happens,",[]],"busolt-s20-010":["(b) a thing from which it follows; and then I define. *A posteriori*, for example: when I define body, I analyse its properties and give a complete definition. The object of an empirical concept cannot be explained [erklären], for everyone already has a complete concept of it: for example, what earth is, what water is, etc. It cannot be done through analysis, since it is pointless to dissect what can be made distinct through explanation; nor through synthesis, for [here] I can only describe and collect concepts. We have empirically given and also empirically made concepts. Empirically given concepts cannot and ought not to be defined; for their marks are not sufficient to give the definition an exhaustive distinctness — they are perhaps only infinitely small parts of it. Description, however, does take place with them. Gold, for example, I cannot define but only describe. In the case of empirically made concepts the *data* are given to us from experience, and from these we form our concept. Such concepts are found where nature is altered: for example, the concept of a house, of a fortress.",[]],"busolt-s20-011":["Every definition, the analytic as well as the synthetic, sets forth the content of the concept. The difference between definition and description consists in this, that the former makes the concept completely distinct, whereas the latter only extends it. Description has to do with the content in so far as we possess it. In the case of empirically made concepts I declare, that is, think, what I intend to say. This takes place through experience, not *a priori*. Concepts *a priori* admit of a definition. In natural science descriptions take place; and when I think several things in one and the same thing, this calls for a declaration.",[]],"busolt-s21-001":["This takes place through analysis. The difficulty here is whether the analysis is also complete. This is very seldom the case. Notwithstanding, many correct *consectaria* [corollaries] can be derived from these imperfect [analyses]. I can indeed know that I know nothing more; but that nothing is lacking no one can know. An analytic definition can therefore never attain apodeictic certainty. From the mathematician I can demand a definition; for through the definition he makes the concept. He can also give it.",[]],"busolt-s21-002":["From the philosopher, on the other hand, I cannot demand it; for he cannot determine whether the analysis is complete. But although he cannot have a complete analysis, he can still have an incomplete one which gives him some marks. So long as such an analysis is incomplete, it is not entitled a definition. The materials of the definition are entitled elementary propositions, that is, incomplete propositions towards a definition. *Definitio est conceptus, qui rem adaequat* [a definition is a concept that is adequate to the thing]. If a concept has distinct marks, but too few of them, it is incomplete; if it has too many marks, it is not precise. A concept which has neither too few nor too many marks, that is, which is adequate to the thing — *conceptus qui adaequat rem* — *est definitio*. A definition can therefore have *completudo*; but if it lacks precision it is still faulty, for something superfluous is stated, and not something neat and precise. Opposed to precision is tautology, in which the same mark occurs twice, though in a concealed manner. For example: God is an infinite, eternal, omniscient being. From the concept 'infinite' all the other properties already follow. In the case of made concepts the definition comes first; in the case of given concepts I first make the concept distinct, I analyse it, and then I give a definition.",[]],"busolt-s21-003":["A definition must be complete. But I can have distinct concepts which, if not complete, are yet incomplete; and then what is required is an exposition of the elementary concepts. Of concepts *a priori* a definition is indeed possible, but not necessary; an *expositio*, however, is. In the case of concepts *a posteriori* there can be not definition but description. A distinction must be drawn between nominal and real definition. A nominal definition is one in which marks are stated in order to discern identity and diversity. This would be an external use of the definition. A real definition, or genetic definition, is when marks are stated in order to distinguish the *objectum* from all others. This would be an internal use of the definition. The nominal and real definition given by Wolff is here criticised. If a definition is to contain the difference [of the thing] from all possible concepts, the marks must be entirely complete. It must therefore be both nominal and real definition. Nominal definitions are therefore not necessary unless a misunderstanding of the expression is to be feared. In the case of Latin terms, however, they are necessary. These terms must be appropriate to the thing and to its genesis. Etymology thus furnishes particular explanations of names. Explanations of names serve merely to distinguish objects from others.",[]],"busolt-s21-004":["Description holds only of the empirical. Definitions which cannot be defined, in so far as they are meant to be objectively sufficient, are themselves an incomplete exposition, and always serve also as a definition *ad instantem usum* [for present use]. In intellectual matters this is admittedly not permissible; yet even here it can be nominal and at the same time an incomplete exposition. For it is always an approximation to definition. For [it is] an incomplete concept, *et hoc sensu definitione procedere potest descriptio* [and in this sense description can precede definition].",[]],"busolt-s21-005":["Note. One can best tell whether one has not [merely] described something when one passes backwards from experience to reason, and can explain genetically *a priori* what one previously had to demonstrate *a posteriori*. Thus, for example, a definition *a priori* of eclipses of the moon could easily be given as soon as it was known that the moon receives its light from the sun, and that the earth sometimes comes between the two. Chemistry composes, and what it composes can then be defined. If, accordingly, I can resolve the object into the *notae* which constitute its essence, these are entitled primary constituents [Urstoffe]. In definition one must ask whether the definition is true as a proposition. For example, 'a man is a man' is certainly true as a proposition. The soul is a thinking being. It is over definition that the greatest disagreement in philosophy is found. For example: virtue is a certain readiness in certain actions. This is not correct. For",[]],"busolt-s21-006":["(a) virtue is a free action,",[]],"busolt-s21-007":["(b) a firm resolve,",[]],"busolt-s21-008":["(c) a steadfast disposition in the exercise of right.",[]],"busolt-s21-009":["Of the nature of things I can say much through analysis. Analytic propositions are certainly true, but at times a great tautology can lurk in them. This is the second thing that must be examined.",[]],"busolt-s21-010":["(3) Whether the concept is also complete. This *completudo* arises from the confluence of all the marks. Time is the order of things in so far as they follow one upon another.",[]],"busolt-s21-011":["(4) Whether the concept is also precise, that is, that no more marks are added than are necessary. The superfluous marks cannot be *compartes* [co-parts] of the others.",[]],"busolt-s21-012":["The cause of the want of precision is that I misplace a mark. To say something *definite* [definitely] means to frame the concept in such a manner that neither more nor less is said than the concept contains. There belongs, therefore, to definition:",[]],"busolt-s21-013":["truth, *completudo* and distinctness. In defining, therefore, I proceed [thus]:",[]],"busolt-s21-014":["(a) I analyse my concept and seek true propositions.",[]],"busolt-s21-015":["(b) The propositions must contain marks through which the concept of the *definitum* becomes distinct.",[]],"busolt-s21-016":["(c) I collect as many marks as are necessary to produce a complete concept",[]],"busolt-s21-017":["A definition of description we cannot give, because this belongs to philosophy; and in philosophy the former is a procedure in which one defines, the latter a procedure in which one describes.",[]],"busolt-s21-018":["When we attempt to explain much in nature, we can tell from this whether the explanation has been understood: [namely] if one takes care [to see] whether the proposition could also be known *a priori* from the very same grounds. An eclipse of the moon can also be explained *a priori* if one knows that the earth comes between the sun and the moon. Further, [one can tell] if one sees whether one can also make the concept. For example, the definition of cinnabar: mercury and sulphur sublimed yield cinnabar; I can therefore both make cinnabar and define it. *Definitio nec sit latior nec sit angustior; seu nec plus, nec minus suo in se continet definito* [let a definition be neither wider nor narrower; that is, let it contain in itself neither more nor less than its *definitum*]. *Definitio ne sit per circulum* [let a definition not be circular] means [that it is faulty when] I try to explain a thing through itself, bringing the *definiendum* into the definition. One has a *circulus vitiosus in demonstrando* when I take as ground the proposition I wish to prove. One also has a *definitum obscurum in definiendo* when I try to use another concept which is likewise obscure to make the other distinct. *Definitio ne sit tautologica*. For example: the soul is a rational being which can judge; here the faculty of judging already lies in 'rational being'. This tautology can be explicit and also implicit.",[]],"busolt-s22-001":["This is distinct from analysis [Zergliederung]. In logical division I have regard to the sphere, that is, to the extent of application of a concept. Logical division is therefore the division of the sphere. The greater the sphere, the more it can be divided. Logical division contains the difference of various concepts in so far as they are contained under a common concept. When all the disjunctive concepts taken together are equal to the *sphaera conceptus*, this is a logically perfect division: for example, triangles are either equilateral",[]],"busolt-s22-002":["or isosceles. The other concepts are entitled *membra dividentia*; they must be equal to the *sphaera conceptus divisi*. There is a codivision and also a subdivision of a *membrum*. Subdivision is the division of a *membrum dividens*: for example, all men are learned or unlearned. Codivisions are therefore divisions in which I divide the concept under different headings. They are side-divisions [Nebeneintheilungen], in which I consider the concept under another sphere. The faults in logical divisions are:",[]],"busolt-s22-003":["(a) when the *membra dividentia* are not opposed to one another;",[]],"busolt-s22-004":["(b) the members of the subdivision must not be brought under the members of the division. For example, 'triangles are equilateral or isosceles': this division is false. One ought to divide them thus: triangles are either equal in all three sides, or unequal. The unequal are either isosceles, having two equal sides, or scalene, where no side is equal. The easiest division is dichotomy. All substances are either bodies or not; this is the foundation of all logical definitions.",[]],"busolt-s23-001":["We know something either immediately or mediately; that is, [in the latter case] we know our modes of knowledge of the object. Such mediate knowledge of the knowledge of the object is a judgment. I can therefore either merely represent something immediately through a concept, or I can have a concept through mediate knowledge. I can thus pass a judgment directly about the object, and through this I can in turn pass a judgment about the knowledge of the object. The relation of one mode of knowledge to another, in so far as the one is the means of the other, is the judgment.",[]],"busolt-s24-001":["All judgments are brought under four titles and divided according to four functions, namely:",[]],"busolt-s24-002":["(a) quantity;",[]],"busolt-s24-003":["(b) quality;",[]],"busolt-s24-004":["(c) relation;",[]],"busolt-s24-005":["(d) modality.",[]],"busolt-s24-006":["Each of these has three *modi*. In respect of quality, judgments are either affirmative, negative or infinite. In respect of quantity, they are either universal, particular or singular. In respect of relation, they are either categorical, hypothetical or disjunctive. And in respect of modality, they are either problematic, assertoric or apodeictic. For example: all men are mortal. This judgment is in quality affirmative, in quantity universal, in relation categorical, in modality assertoric. The judgments of the first two titles are readily distinguished.",[]],"busolt-s24-007":["(1) But how are judgments of relation distinguished? We have three kinds of relation:",[]],"busolt-s24-008":["(a) of the subject to the predicate, that is, categorical;",[]],"busolt-s24-009":["(b) of the ground to the consequence, that is, hypothetical;",[]],"busolt-s24-010":["(c) the relation of the members to one another and to the divided concept, that is, disjunctive. The relation of the members to one another is [one in which] they are opposed: all men are either virtuous or vicious.",[]],"busolt-s24-011":["(2) Judgments of modality.",[]],"busolt-s24-012":["If I hold a judgment to be possible, and accept it merely as a touchstone [to see] what might follow from it, this is a problematic judgment. A judgment with consciousness of its truth is assertoric. A judgment which is necessarily true is apodeictic. For example, if I say 'the moon may well have inhabitants', this is problematic; if I say 'it has inhabitants', this is assertoric; and were I to say 'it must have inhabitants', the judgment would be apodeictic. In problematic judgments, then, the consequence remains clear, while the ground, or the one premiss, is undecided. A judgment is therefore a knowledge of a knowledge, or a mediate knowledge of the object; for every concept is merely a mode of knowledge. For example, 'living' and 'creatures' are concepts, but 'men are living creatures' is a judgment. In defining judgment I cannot consider the concept alone; for there are judgments in which only the relation of two concepts is required — this is the categorical — but in others the relation of two or more judgments is also required, namely in the hypothetical and disjunctive. The first definition, that a judgment is a knowledge of a knowledge, therefore remains the best. That the definition of judgment is not so evident is due to the fact that we are already judging when we define.",[]],"busolt-s24-013":["Note. All acts of the understanding issue in judgments, and every object that we know, every concept, is as it were a predicate of a possible judgment. The understanding, which was previously explained as the faculty of concepts, can thus be explained as the faculty of judgments, or of rules. For the understanding is the source of rules, since every judgment is a rule and every rule a judgment. For example: all men are mortal. As soon as I see a man, this proposition is as it were a rule for me: this man too must die. This very definition of the understanding agrees with the definition given by Wolff, namely that the understanding is the faculty of distinct representations; for it is only through judgments that the logical distinctness of representations arises. Accordingly, distinct representations ought to be discussed under judgments, since a distinct concept already presupposes a judgment. A distinct concept is possible, namely, through a clear mark; and the representation [of the mark] amounts to the predicate of a judgment. The representation of the object itself is the subject; consequently I have *materiam scilicet dati iudicii* [the matter, namely, of a given judgment]. The concept 'a man' is clear; 'a rational man', distinct. For here I represent the man by means of the predicate 'rational', and so have a judgment. Distinctness is twofold:",[]],"busolt-s24-014":["(a) in the concept; this is logical [distinctness]; *haec respicit intellectum* [it has regard to the understanding];",[]],"busolt-s24-015":["(b) in the intuition; this is aesthetic [distinctness]; *sententia haec respicit sensus* [this has regard to the senses].",[]],"busolt-s24-016":["We can have aesthetic distinctness of things while lacking logical distinctness. For example, a servant standing in a palace has indeed a sensitive distinctness of it; but since he does not know what cornices or columns are, he lacks logical distinctness. Logical distinctness properly belongs only to the philosopher. In every judgment there occur matter and form:",[]],"busolt-s24-017":["(a) the matter, that is, the mode of knowledge by means of which I know an *objectum*;",[]],"busolt-s24-018":["(b) the form, that is, the relation of the modes of knowledge. For example, in 'man is mortal', 'man' and 'mortal' are the matter, while the relation of man to mortality is the form.",[]],"busolt-s24-019":["Since logic abstracts from all matter, there remains to it only the form; and this is considered in a fourfold respect, as has just been stated. The essential form of judgments consists in relation; for concepts [too] have sphere, quality and modality, but relation is peculiar to the *iudicium*. Categorical judgments underlie the others. For hypothetical judgments consist of two categorical judgments, of which I regard the one as the ground, the other as the consequence. Disjunctive judgments consist of several categorical judgments. The disjunctive [members] taken together are equal to the *conceptus divisus*.",[]],"busolt-s24-020":["Since, therefore, the *iudicia categorica* are the *data iudiciorum hypotheticorum et disiunctivorum*, logicians regarded the former as the only true judgments, and said that the others could be reduced to them. When the author speaks of subject, predicate and copula, this applies only to categorical judgments; for in the hypothetical we must distinguish *antecedens* and *consequens*, and in the disjunctive the *membra dividentia* and the *conceptus divisus*. We shall therefore speak [of]",[]],"busolt-s25-001":["These contain two concepts of things, and in them there is distinguished:",[]],"busolt-s25-002":["(a) the subject, which is the concept of which the other is a mark; or *est conceptus, quem per alium cognitio* [?the concept which is known through another];",[]],"busolt-s25-003":["(b) the predicate, which is a concept that is a mark of the other; or *est conceptus per quem subiectum repraesento cognitione* [?the concept through which I represent the subject in knowledge];",[]],"busolt-s25-004":["(c) the copula, which is that whereby the relation of the *conceptus* is signified.",[]],"busolt-s25-005":["Categorical judgments are divided *quoad qualitatem* [in respect of quality] into *affirmativa*, *negativa* and *infinita*:",[]],"busolt-s25-006":["(a) *iudicium affirmativum*, when the copula *est* expresses the connection of subject and predicate.",[]],"busolt-s25-007":["b.) A *judicium negativum* [negative judgment] is one in which the copula *non est* [is not] expresses the opposition of subject and predicate, that is, the negation of the judgment. It is therefore expressed through the negation of the relation, and the negation must accordingly stand with the copula and not with the predicate. For if the latter is done, the concept is indeed negative, but not the judgment; logic, in dealing with judgments, has regard solely to the relation.",[]],"busolt-s25-008":["A *judicium infinitum* [infinite judgment] is one in which the predicate is negated. *Quoad quantitatem* [as regards quantity, judgments are divided] into *universalia, particularia et singularia* [universal, particular and singular].",[]],"busolt-s25-009":["1.) A *judicium universale* [universal judgment] is one in which the predicate is predicated of the whole sphere [*sphaera*].",[]],"busolt-s25-010":["2.) A *judicium particulare* [particular judgment] is one in which the predicate is predicated of a part of the sphere.",[]],"busolt-s25-011":["3.) A *judicium singulare* [singular judgment] is one in which the subject has no sphere, and in which the predicate is therefore predicated of the whole subject.",[]],"busolt-s25-012":["Singular judgments [*judicia singularia*] can be regarded as universal judgments [*judicia universalia*], in so far as the predicate holds of the subject without exception; and for this reason most [logicians] rightly omit singular judgments. We also speak of *generalia* and *specialia*; but *generalia* are not *universalia*, nor are *specialia* *particularia*. For example, to expound geography *generaliter* [in general terms] is not to expound it *universaliter* [universally]; for geography holds only *particulariter* [particularly], namely, of the earth. *Generalia* are what pertain to the genus, *specialia* what pertain to the species. Some judgments are so constituted that the concept of the subject must be restricted if the judgment is to be valid. For example, ‘All the angles in a triangle together amount to 180 degrees’; but this holds only of rectilinear, not of curvilinear, triangles. Similarly, ‘All bodies are heavy’. But if there were no other body by which a body could be governed, how could it be heavy? This concept also must therefore be restricted, [namely] to the relation to another body. In such judgments, then, the subject, or rather the universality of the subject, must be restricted, in order that the predicate may belong to it. Judgments of this kind, in which the assertion is conditioned, are entitled conditioned judgments. If, however, the condition is attached not to the subject but to the copula, the judgment is entitled a *judicium modale* [modal judgment]; for example, ‘All bodies are contingently heavy’. From this arises what is called the *modalitas judiciorum* [modality of judgments], under which come possibility, actuality and necessity. Remark. This modality is of the highest importance; for how often are possibility and necessity confused with one another, as, for example, in the demonstration of the immortality of the soul. To express judgments of quality and quantity letters have been chosen. These are of no real use here, but only in syllogistic, namely: *Asserit A, negat E, sed universaliter ambo; asserit I, negat O, sed particulariter ambo* [A affirms, E denies, but both universally; I affirms, O denies, but both particularly].",[]],"busolt-s25-013":["Logicians express modality by the question *Quae propositio?* [Which proposition?], quality by *Qualis est propositio?* [Of what kind is the proposition?], and quantity by *Quanta est propositio?* [How great is the proposition?].",[]],"busolt-s25-014":["So far we have spoken of simple judgments; but there are also compound judgments. For example, ‘God, the soul and all finite",[]],"busolt-s25-015":["spirits are immortal’. Compound judgments rest on this, that either",[]],"busolt-s25-016":["a.) [they are compound as to the] subject, or",[]],"busolt-s25-017":["b.) [they are compound as to the] predicate.",[]],"busolt-s26-001":["Hypothetical judgments consist of two judgments which stand in a relation of consequence; and to them belong",[]],"busolt-s26-002":["a. the antecedent, or that which precedes the consequence; this is the ground.",[]],"busolt-s26-003":["b. the consequent, or that which follows from the antecedent; this is the consequence. They thus have a relation of connection [*nexus*] of judgments. Disjunctive judgments consist of two or more judgments which stand in a relation of opposition. This opposition, however, must be contradictory: the members [*membra*] must be contradictory, and yet together equal to the divided concept [*conceptus divisus*; the text reads *diverso*].",[]],"busolt-s26-004":["An exponible judgment is one which is at once affirmative and negative. In German the words ‘alone’, ‘only’, ‘few’, etc., serve for such judgments. For example, ‘Few men are pious’ [means] ‘Some men are pious; some are not pious’. Aesthetic judgments are distinguished by us from logical judgments by there being, besides the concepts, a sensation as well; for example, ‘Oh! How many men are godless!’ This is an aesthetic judgment. If we take a proposition as practical, we can say either that it is formally practical, and then it is opposed to the theoretical, or that it is materially practical, and then it is opposed to the speculative. Practical propositions in respect of form are those according to which an execution is possible. Here the relation in question is not that of modes of knowledge but that of freedom. These practical propositions are opposed to theoretical propositions. For example, ‘A triangle consists of three straight lines’ is theoretical; ‘To describe a triangle from three given straight lines’ is formally practical. Of those practical propositions which are opposed to the speculative, logic knows nothing.",[]],"busolt-s26-005":["Knowledge that is theoretical in form, but from which many propositions can be derived as to what I ought to do, or which contains many imperatives, is practical in respect of its matter; for example, ‘There is a single Judge of the world’. Logic, however, which abstracts from all content, knows nothing of the fact that some modes of knowledge are so fruitful. The practical propositions in logic must be such as these: to draw an inference from given judgments and a *terminus medius* [middle term]. The author speaks of demonstrable and indemonstrable judgments. Demonstrable judgments are those which must be preceded by another judgment, or which, to confirm their truth, require a proof. Indemonstrable judgments are those which need no proof whatsoever, but are already true in themselves. The former are mediately certain, the latter immediately certain judgments. We often say that a proposition is immediately certain when it is not so. One must therefore at first take a judgment to be mediately certain, because we",[]],"busolt-s26-006":["a.) may well feel within us the result of the proof without having the proof itself.",[]],"busolt-s26-007":["b.) Because the human understanding is more inclined to take something as mediately certain [*sic*: the sense seems to require ‘immediately’]. For it always wants to go further. We must therefore handle these propositions with care, and first examine them closely.",[]],"busolt-s26-008":["The author calls an inexplicable [*unerforschliches*] knowledge empty, because by it our knowledge neither gains nor loses anything. But a knowledge can be",[]],"busolt-s26-009":["a.) empty of sense, that is, a proposition which is empty in respect of its matter;",[]],"busolt-s26-010":["b.) empty in respect of the form of the understanding; for example, ‘Man has a human nature’.",[]],"busolt-s26-011":["These propositions, which explain *idem per idem* [the same by the same], advance knowledge neither analytically nor synthetically. They are tautological propositions: through them I gain neither an increase in distinctness nor any addition to knowledge. Not all identical propositions, however, are empty propositions, but only the tautological. An identical proposition which can be discerned only through analysis is not to be regarded as empty, for it helps towards distinctness. A proposition is empty in respect of its content when its representations relate to no subject. The identity in a tautology is known immediately, whereas identity proper is known mediately, that is, through the analysis of the concepts. These tautological propositions neither help nor harm. Yet we perceive even what is true as absurd, if it serves none of our purposes. Negative propositions do not indeed extend our modes of knowledge, but they remedy errors, whereas false propositions do harm; for example, ‘The soul is not composite’. If I employ negatively something which no one has ever called in question, the result is absurd.",[]],"busolt-s26-012":["*Propositiones indemonstrabiles* [indemonstrable propositions] are hypothetical [*sic*; perhaps ‘synthetic’] propositions which have arisen not through analysis of the subject but from some other source. Synthetic propositions *a priori*, when they are intuitive *a priori*, are *axiomata* [axioms]. Philosophy does not have false judgments *a priori* [the reading is doubtful]; but in so far as it is discursive, philosophy may well have *principia* [principles, as distinct from] *axiomata*. ‘Axiom’ means as much as a dignity [*Würde*] which is evident in itself and need not be proved; it carries certainty with it. The author deals with *judicia intuitiva* and *discursiva* [intuitive and discursive judgments].",[]],"busolt-s26-013":["a.) Judgments are *judicia intuitiva* [intuitive judgments] when they represent something concrete *in singulari* [in the singular].",[]],"busolt-s26-014":["These are either",[]],"busolt-s26-015":["1.) intuitive *a priori*, that is, pure intuitive concepts (*judicia*) [judgments]: mathematical; or",[]],"busolt-s26-016":["2.) *a posteriori*, empirical intuitive *judicia* [judgments].",[]],"busolt-s26-017":["A *consectarium* is a proposition of inference, especially in philosophy. A *corollarium* is a proposition from which another is inferred by an inference, especially in mathematics. A corollary is a monosyllogistic ratiocination [*ratiocinatio monosyllogistica*]. A theorem is a theoretical proposition in so far as it is capable of, and in need of, a proof. It is nothing other than a polysyllogistic ratiocination [*ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*], that is, a proposition inferred from other preceding inferences. The requisites of a theorem are:",[]],"busolt-s26-018":["a. the proposition;",[]],"busolt-s26-019":["b. the demonstration.",[]],"busolt-s26-020":["A *problema* [problem] is a practical proposition, distinct from the foregoing. For the requisites of a problem are:",[]],"busolt-s26-021":["a.) the *quaestio problematis* [question of the problem], by which it is set out what ought to be done;",[]],"busolt-s26-022":["b.) the *resolutio quaestionis* [solution of the question], [setting out] in what way it ought to be done;",[]],"busolt-s26-023":["c.) the *demonstratio* [demonstration] that this solution is true.",[]],"busolt-s26-024":["These problems belong especially to mathematics; yet there are also special ones, as in morals, in medicine, [and in the doctrine] of the human soul.",[]],"busolt-s26-025":["It is therefore a very important rule *ne sit tautologica problematis resolutio*, that is, *ne resolutionem praedicamus in definitionem* [that the solution of a problem be not tautological, that is, that we do not state the solution in the definition]. In morals, as has been said, there are all too many such problematic solutions. This arises from the fact that morals already actually exists as a canon, that is, we actually have rules by which we direct the judgments of the understanding and our appraisals of moral actions; but not as an organon. That is to say, morals has not yet been so expounded that the concepts of the understanding influence my will and inclination, so that virtue is not merely approved but also practised. In the tautological solution of a problem one analyses the definition itself. In morals the treatment need not be strictly precise; but in mathematics there can be no superfluity. For although precision is otherwise an essential property of a system, certain sciences are nevertheless exempted from it, and to these belong, besides morals, the physical sciences also.",[]],"busolt-s26-026":["1. Definitions, axioms, corollaries, theorems with their demonstrations, and problems with their solutions and demonstrations: these are the parts of a system, and indeed of a dogmatic system.",[]],"busolt-s26-027":["To such a dogmatic system there belong, however:",[]],"busolt-s26-028":["a.) *partes domesticae* [domestic parts], which constitute the system proper;",[]],"busolt-s26-029":["b.) *partes peregrinae* [foreign parts], which are drawn as it were from another field; as when in moral philosophy [something is taken] concerning the existence of God, or as in metaphysics examples are taken from physics. A part of this kind is called a *lemma*, which is a foreign part of a system, drawn from another science. [The Latin of the last clause is corrupt.]",[]],"busolt-s26-030":["2.) *Partes essentiales et extraessentiales* [essential and extra-essential parts].",[]],"busolt-s26-031":["a.) Essential parts are those whose removal removes the whole system.",[]],"busolt-s26-032":["b.) Extra-essential parts are those by which [the system] is illustrated; this foreign part of the system does not extend it. Of this kind is the *notio* [the text reads *Notion*; perhaps *scholion*]. This is, properly, the resting-place of the system: in it one mentions the history and the utility of the system, and refutes its opponents. These parts are for the most part aesthetic.",[]],"busolt-s26-033":["In logic a *qualitas occulta* [occult quality; the text reads *Qualitaet nulla*] is said to be present where the confession of the inquirer’s ignorance is taken for the definition of the thing itself and the discovery of its cause; for example, ‘What is the cause of the propagation of animals and plants?’ Answer: ‘A *vis plastica* [formative power]’. Here I define *idem per idem*, and it is as much as to say: the cause of this effect is hidden. What does a mode of knowledge gain from such an answer?",[]],"busolt-s26-034":["Remark. An occult quality is a tautological explanation of appearances, for it has regard to the subject, not to the object. Of this kind is Lambert’s *idiosyncrasia*, of which he says that a man, near to whom a dog and a snake [are kept], has a disposition averse to them. [The Latin here is corrupt and the sense uncertain.] And indeed *idiosyncrasia* is a quality which influences this or that individual. Of the very same kind is the word, so called in recent times, *bon sens* (sound understanding). For who has given a definition of *sensus communis* [common sense], unless a tautological one? Even the Crusian philosophy rests on a principle which is nothing but a *qualitas occulta*, namely: what I cannot but find true, is true. The Newtonian philosophy, which is based on the principle of the reciprocal sphere [of action] in the connection of the world’s course, takes the term *vis attractiva* [attractive force] as a *qualitas occulta* in the physical sense, that is, merely as a confession of ignorance of the true causes, and not as the true cause itself.",[]],"busolt-s26-035":["If one judgment is inferred from another without an intermediate judgment, this is an inference of the understanding; if *per judicium intermedium* [through an intermediate judgment], an inference of reason. For example, ‘All men are mortal; therefore Caius also is mortal’. Here Caius is a concept which I did not previously have; the inference is therefore false, and I must infer only mediately, by forming for myself a concept of Caius. For a *consequentia immediata* [immediate inference] the *judicium praevium* [prior judgment] and the *conclusio* [the text reads *exclusio*] must contain [the same] concept. They are distinguished in no other way than in quality, quantity and relation. Immediate inferences take place",[]],"busolt-s26-036":["a.) *per judicia aequipollentia* [through equipollent judgments];",[]],"busolt-s26-037":["b.) *per judicia subalternata* [through subalternate judgments];",[]],"busolt-s26-038":["c.) *per judicia opposita* [through opposed judgments];",[]],"busolt-s26-039":["d.) *per conversionem judiciorum* [through the conversion of judgments];",[]],"busolt-s26-040":["e.) *per contrapositionem judiciorum* [through the contraposition of judgments].",[]],"busolt-s26-041":["The first kind may be dropped. It is empty, since to infer from one judgment another which is identical with the first is as much as nothing; it remains always the same judgment. By the second kind I infer from the universal to the particular. By the third kind the immediate inference is made *per judicia opposita*. This opposition is threefold.",[]],"busolt-s26-042":["a.) Contradictory. Here, of two judgments only one can be true, and I therefore infer: if one is true, the other is false, and conversely. For this it is required that one be universal affirmative and the other particular negative, or that one be universal negative and the other particular affirmative. For example, ‘All men are mortal’, ‘Some are not mortal’; ‘No substance is simple’, ‘Some are simple’.",[]],"busolt-s26-043":["b.) Subcontrary, where one judgment is particular affirmative and the other particular negative; for example, ‘Some men are evil’ – ‘Some are not evil’.",[]],"busolt-s26-044":["c.) Contrary, when to a universal affirmative judgment a universal negative judgment is opposed, or conversely. For example, ‘All men are vicious’. Someone may be so shocked by such a proposition as to say: ‘No man is vicious’. Here he does more than is needed to contradict the other. Contrary opposed judgments [*judicia contraria opposita*] can both be false, because in them more is denied than is necessary; the falsity therefore lies in the universality. In the following kinds subject and predicate are transposed. But in conversion one can change the quantity, though not the quality. The transposition of the terms is entitled *metathesis terminorum*. Through it an immediate inference arises: through the conversion of judgments, where the quantity and not the quality is altered; and through the contraposition of judgments, where the quality and not the quantity is altered. For example, ‘All men are mortal; therefore some mortals are men’: this is by conversion. ‘What is not mortal is not a man’: this is by contraposition. In the conversion of judgments, the judgment which is to be converted is entitled the *judicium conversum*, and that through which the conversion arises the *judicium convertens*. Conversion takes place either *per accidens* or *simpliciter*.",[]],"busolt-s26-045":["The conditions in respect of form are these:",[]],"busolt-s26-046":["a.) Universal affirmative propositions can be converted only *per accidens*; universal negative propositions only *simpliciter*; and thirdly, particular affirmative or negative propositions also *simpliciter*. The first means that the *judicium conversum* is universal, and of it I can now say conversely, ‘Some mortals are men’. For I think mortals, among whom men also are; that is, the subject ‘man’ is under the sphere of mortals, as the predicate. The predicate must therefore be greater than the subject, and I can accordingly take some mortals to be men. There are universal affirmative propositions which can be converted *simpliciter*, namely those in which the sphere of the subject and of the predicate is equal. For example, ‘All figures enclosed in three straight lines are triangles’; ‘All triangles are figures enclosed in three straight lines’. [Here the text continues ‘are triangles’, apparently by error.] This is a subtlety, and I here leave it to each to decide how many triangles he will take out of an infinite multitude. This distinction, however, is *evanescens* [vanishing]. All negative judgments which contain a conflict can be converted *simpliciter*; for example, ‘No triangle is without angles’, ‘No figure without angles is a triangle’. All particular affirmative or negative propositions can be converted *simpliciter*, that is, in such a way that the quantity is not altered. Contraposition means this: every universal proposition can be contraposed. For example, ‘All men are mortal’, ‘Non-mortals are not men’. How is inference made according to *judicia mediata* [mediate judgments]? If one concept is to be inferred from another in which it is not contained, a concept which contains the condition of the rule must be added. The concept of the object now contains the condition of the rule, and therefore the predicate of the conclusion [*sic*: perhaps ‘of the rule’] must hold of this [object]. For example, ‘All men are mortal; Caius is a man; therefore Caius is mortal’. This inference of reason is based on the principle: to everything, in so far as it can be subsumed under the condition, the predicate belongs. An inference of reason, however, is *cognitio a priori per subsumptionem conceptus sub conditione regulae cuiusdam generalis* [knowledge *a priori* through the subsumption of a concept under the condition of some general rule].",[]],"busolt-s26-047":["The inference of reason has two requisites:",[]],"busolt-s26-048":["a.) a universal rule, that is, the major [premiss];",[]],"busolt-s26-049":["b.) the subsumption of a concept under the condition of this universal rule, that is, the minor [premiss];",[]],"busolt-s26-050":["c.) the connection of the predicate with the subject, or that which belongs [to the subject] from the universal rule through the subsumption: this is the conclusion. The major and the minor are entitled premisses. The premisses and the conclusion are thus what belongs to the inference.",[]],"busolt-s26-051":["In these inferences regard is had to relation, and according to it inferences are divided into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive. All inferences are judged according to the major proposition: if it is a categorical judgment, the inference is also categorical. Only in categorical inferences are there subject, predicate and *terminus medius* [middle term]; and hence one readily sees the importance of the saying that three terms are required for an inference of reason: the major, or the predicate of the conclusion; the minor, or the subject of the conclusion; and the *terminus medius*, which is the condition of the conclusion. Every inference of reason in general consists of three parts:",[]],"busolt-s26-052":["a.) premisses;",[]],"busolt-s26-053":["b.) major and minor; and",[]],"busolt-s26-054":["c.) conclusion.",[]],"busolt-s26-055":["The matter of inferences of reason consists of the premisses, or the propositions from which the conclusion follows. The form consists in the derivation of the conclusion from the premisses. An inference of reason can be correct in its matter but faulty in its form. Only in categorical inferences is there a *medius terminus*. In hypothetical inferences there is merely the relation of two judgments, [namely] how one follows from the correctness of the other.",[]],"busolt-s27-001":["Others count the principle of contradiction as the chief rule of inferences of reason: no judgment is true in which the predicate contradicts the subject. But this *principium* of contradiction cannot be employed as [the principle of] an inference of reason; for it is only a *principium judicii* [principle of judgment].",[]],"busolt-s27-002":["An inference of reason is affirmative when the conclusion is affirmative, and negative when the conclusion is negative. In accordance with this general division of all inferences of reason we lay down two special rules:",[]],"busolt-s27-003":["a.) *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius* [a mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself]; this holds of affirmative judgments.",[]],"busolt-s27-004":["b.) *Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi* [what conflicts with a mark conflicts with the thing itself]; this holds of negative inferences. For example, ‘To be alterable is a mark of everything divisible; to be divisible is a mark of a body; therefore, by means of divisibility, alterability is a mark of body’. [The example illustrates rule a.]",[]],"busolt-s27-005":["Every conclusion contains subject and predicate. The *terminus medius* occurs in both premisses: in the major, therefore, there will be predicate and *terminus medius*, in the minor subject and *terminus medius*. In such inferences of reason only the *terminus medius* can be transposed. The mode of transposition is fourfold, and hence arise the four figures of syllogistic.",[]],"busolt-s27-006":["*Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi*. For example, ‘Nothing composite is simple; all matter is composite; therefore no matter is simple’, etc. ‘To be simple’ [is denied] of the composite, and by virtue of the composite, of bodies. The two rules cited are precisely what the ancients called the *dictum de omni et nullo*.",[]],"busolt-s27-007":["a.) *Dictum de omni*: whatever holds of the genus or species holds also of all individuals contained in the genus or species.",[]],"busolt-s27-008":["b.) *Dictum de nullo*: whatever conflicts with the genus or species conflicts also with all individuals contained in the genus or species.",[]],"busolt-s27-009":["The author explains the principle of hypothetical and disjunctive inferences in §§ 364 and 365. There are thus only three principles of inferences of reason; for the *consequentiae immediatae* [immediate inferences] are not properly inferences of reason. They are only consequences, and need no rule. The author speaks of ordinary and extraordinary inferences of reason. But there is no other difference here than that the inferences in which the form is concealed are extraordinary.",[]],"busolt-s28-001":["Figure I is too well known to need explanation; it is as it were the root of every transposition of the middle term in the other [figures].",[]],"busolt-s28-002":["Figure II. Here the middle term is the predicate in the major and the minor. For example, ‘No thinking being is composite; all bodies are composite; therefore no body is a thinking being’.",[]],"busolt-s28-003":["Figure III. Here the middle term is the subject in the major and minor proposition. For example, ‘All men are imperfect; some men are virtuous; therefore some virtuous [beings] are imperfect’.",[]],"busolt-s28-004":["Figure IV. Here the middle term is the predicate in the major and the subject in the minor. For example, ‘Whatever has the power of acting is spirit; every spirit is substance; therefore every substance has the power of acting’.",[]],"busolt-s29-001":["1.) In an ordinary inference of reason there can be no more than three principal concepts, for the conclusion has only two. Remark. If one of the two principal concepts in the premisses is ambiguous, the ambiguity is found in the whole inference. For example, ‘In the place where I am there is no one else; the room is the place where I am; therefore there is no one else in the room’.",[]],"busolt-s29-002":["2.) The middle term must not enter into the conclusion; this indeed is the worst fault that can occur.",[]],"busolt-s29-003":["3.) *Ex puris negativis nihil sequitur* [from purely negative premisses nothing follows]. This is seen by sound reason; it follows from the nature of the case itself. For the subsumption must be under the condition of the rule: I must say that there belongs to a thing that which is the condition of the rule, and this must surely be affirmative. For example, ‘No man is an irrational animal; no ox is a man; therefore no ox is an irrational animal’.",[]],"busolt-s29-004":["4.) *Ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur* [from purely particular premisses nothing follows]. For otherwise what stands under the condition of the rule cannot be brought under the predicate of the rule. For example, ‘Some sentient beings are men; some irrational animals are sentient beings; therefore some irrational animals are men’.",[]],"busolt-s29-005":["5.) *Conclusio sequi debet partem debiliorem* [the conclusion must follow the weaker part]. N.B. Among the premisses, that which is either particular or negative is entitled the *pars debilior*. The proposition runs: if one premiss is negative, the conclusion is negative; if one premiss is particular, the conclusion is particular. Ground: if I have subsumed only some things under the condition of the universal rule, the predicate of the universal rule must also hold only of some. If the rule is negative, the negation of the rule must also hold of what stands under the rule.",[]],"busolt-s29-006":["6.) The conclusion contains neither more nor less than the premisses; that is, no wider mark [*notus*, the reading is doubtful] is to be found in the conclusion. Nothing more must therefore be inferred than is comprised in the premisses. In the premisses there are the major, the minor and the *terminus medius*; in the conclusion only the major and the minor. The first figure is immediately evident. In the second and third figures one must first reflect what the consequence would be. The fourth figure logicians have entitled the unnatural; in it they have no doubt noticed the complication. In the last figures the form can be very much concealed.",[]],"busolt-s29-007":["The rules of the first figure are:",[]],"busolt-s29-008":["a.) The major must be universal.",[]],"busolt-s29-009":["b.) The minor [must be] affirmative.",[]],"busolt-s29-010":["These rules seem to restrict the first figure; but they show us, rather, its extension to each and every kind of inference. The major must by the rule be universal, but it can be either affirmative or negative; the minor is to be affirmative, but it can be either universal or particular. In the first figure, therefore, inferences of every quality and quantity are possible, and so of A, E, I and O. In the others only some of these are possible in each case. Consequently the first figure is the genuine, the only natural and pure [figure]. Remark. The above rules hold for each and every inference of reason. For in every inference of reason there must always be a rule, and a rule is nothing other than a universal truth. Further, since in every inference of reason I am to subsume a concept, I must assert that this concept belongs under a universal rule, and so must affirm.",[]],"busolt-s29-011":["The rules of the second figure restrict themselves solely to one kind of inference, namely, to negative conclusions.",[]],"busolt-s29-012":["The third figure can have only particular conclusions.",[]],"busolt-s29-013":["In the fourth figure only particular or negative conclusions are possible; the conclusion thus has only *partes debiliores*. From this it is evident that the first figure alone is pure, and that all the others contain mixed inferences. A *ratiocinium purum* [pure ratiocination] is one into which no proposition other than the major, the minor and the conclusion enters.",[]],"busolt-s29-014":["A *ratiocinium impurum seu hybridum* [impure or hybrid ratiocination] is one in which, in addition to the three propositions, an immediate inference [*consequentia immediata*] must also be thought. It is thus a coupling of a ratiocination with an immediate inference, and occurs in the three last figures. The explanation in the case of the second figure: ‘No thinking being is composite; all bodies are composite; therefore no body is a thinking substance’. Here, in considering the major, I think to myself *per conversionem*: ‘Nothing composite is a thinking substance’. I thus bring the second figure into the first *per conversionem majoris*. The conclusion here must be negative. For the second [figure] is to be brought into the first by conversion, and in the first the major must be universal. Since only negative propositions can yield a universal proposition by simple conversion [*per conversionem simpliciter*], the conclusion also must be negative: *conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*. [The construction of the last sentence is irregular and has been smoothed.]",[]],"busolt-s29-015":["Explanation of the third figure: ‘All men are imperfect; some are virtuous; therefore some virtuous [beings] are imperfect’. Here the *terminus medius* does not stand in its proper place, and only the reduction to the first figure gives it this. ‘All men are imperfect; some virtuous [beings] are men; therefore some virtuous [beings] are imperfect’. In the reduction of the third figure, therefore, the *metathesis terminorum* produces the minor, and does so through an immediate inference: from ‘Some virtuous [beings] are men’ follows ‘Some men are virtuous’. The proof that in the third figure only particular conclusions occur lies in this. I must here be able to reduce the minor to the first figure, and this minor must be affirmative. It is therefore necessary, since only particular propositions can be converted *simpliciter* by immediate inference, that the minor proposition also be a particular proposition, and that the inference then be governed by it as the *pars debilior*. Consequently the conclusion in the third figure must always be particular.",[]],"busolt-s29-016":["In the fourth figure the major is made the minor. From this it is now evident that the three last modes of inference are unnatural and merely confuse the understanding, and must therefore be banished from logic, which ought to be a lighthouse of the mind [*Seele*]. Every judgment has a *modus* which expresses its quantity and quality; this is what the vowels A, E, I, O signify. Of these four vowels only three can occur in a syllogism, since there are only three propositions.",[]],"busolt-s29-017":["From the permutation of these vowels arise sixty-four particular forms. But by the rule *ex puris negativis nihil sequitur* sixteen drop out; by *conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem* twenty-four drop out. In all only nineteen are possible, and among these some are corrupt, so that only [a number is lacking in the text] remain. To bring these into formulae a certain technique of technical words has been devised. To the vowels consonants have also been added, which, however, are likewise all arbitrary. The rules of the vowels [*sic*: properly ‘consonants’] are:",[]],"busolt-s29-018":["a.) S signifies that [the proposition] is to be converted *simpliciter*, P *per accidens*. M signifies that [the premisses] are to be transposed; this takes place *per metathesin*, *per migrationem*.",[]],"busolt-s29-019":["b.) C [signifies that the reduction is] to be carried out *per impossibile*, by contraposition. The technical words are entitled:",[]],"busolt-s29-020":["In the first figure, Barbara, Celarent, Darii, Ferio. In the second figure, Cesare, Camestres, Festino, Baroco. In the third figure, Darapti, Felapton, Disamis, Datisi, Bocardo, Ferison. In the fourth figure, Calemes, Bamalip, Dimatis, Fesapo, Fresison. All that has hitherto been said of syllogisms holds only of categorical inferences. Hypothetical ratiocinations are of an entirely different kind. *Ratiocinia hypothetica* have two modes:",[]],"busolt-s29-021":["a.) The *modus ponens*. Here I infer *a veritate antecedentis ad consequentis positionem* [from the truth of the antecedent to the positing of the consequent].",[]],"busolt-s29-022":["b.) The *modus tollens*. Here I infer *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis* [from the removal of the consequent to the removal of the antecedent]; for example, ‘If there is blind chance, there is no order in the world; but the latter is false; therefore the former’ [is false]. This is the *modus tollens*.",[]],"busolt-s29-023":["‘If bodies are divisible, they are alterable; but the former is true; therefore the latter’. The major proposition says nothing more than how one proposition follows from the other. If the consequence is incorrect, it is said to be an inference *a baculo ad angulum* [from the stick to the corner]. The *modus tollens* holds only from the consequent to the antecedent, namely, *sublato consequente tollitur antecedens* [if the consequent is removed, the antecedent is removed]; but from the positing of the consequent to the positing of the antecedent the *modus ponens* is not valid. Disjunctive inferences of reason consist in this, that the major is divided into several parts which are opposed to one another.",[]],"busolt-s29-024":["The *membra dividentia* [dividing members] we call, in an inference of reason, *membra disjunctiva*. In such an inference we infer *a negatione omnium membrorum disjunctionis praeter unum ad positionem huius unius* [from the negation of all the members of the disjunction except one to the positing of this one].",[]],"busolt-s29-025":["With two members one infers *a negatione unius ad positionem alterius* [from the negation of one to the positing of the other]: if one is false, the other is true. Or I infer also *a positione unius membri disjunctionis ad negationem ceterorum omnium* [from the positing of one member of the disjunction to the negation of all the rest].",[]],"busolt-s29-026":["A *dilemma* is that kind of argument in which the major is hypothetical. All dilemmas have a negative conclusion; for example, ‘If God had not created the best world, then he either could not or would not have created it’. Another [kind of] syllogism is called *cornutus* [the horned] [or] *crocodilus* [the crocodile]. The ancients made much of the dilemma, when they drew further consequences from [others’] assertions. [The sentence is incomplete in the text.] A dilemma always has something sophistical about it, and is an indirect mode of inference [*modus indirectus*].",[]],"busolt-s29-027":["One mode of inference is:",[]],"busolt-s29-028":["From the consequence to the ground. For the proposition is then not [an] affirmation [*sic*: perhaps ‘not valid’], since a consequence can have more than one ground. Only then can I infer truly according to this mode, when I establish the truth of all the consequences. To infer from the falsity of the consequences to the falsity of the ground is quite easy; for I need only show that one consequence is false. The dilemma has much that is convenient about it; for",[]],"busolt-s29-029":["a.) one does not prove directly, from the constitution of the thing;",[]],"busolt-s29-030":["b.) negative propositions also carry more force. They serve as it were for persuasion. A contradiction always makes a greater relative impression; it is far more striking than a true proof. What holds of immediate consequences has just been stated. Every ratiocination is either *formale* [formal] or *crypticum*, a concealed inference of reason. For the most part we speak in *ratiocinia cryptica*; for example, ‘The soul, because it is simple, is imperishable’. A *ratiocinium formale* is one that is in due scholastic form. In the understanding every ratiocination is formal, though not always in the words. An *enthymema* is entitled an inference in which one of the premisses is omitted; for example, ‘All men can err; therefore I [can err]’.",[]],"busolt-s29-031":["Inferences by induction and by analogy. Inferences of reason are either apodictic or not. What I know through reason is apodictic. Through reason I know everything that is necessary; what I know through experience, on the other hand, is not apodictic. Inferences of the understanding yield merely an empirical certainty. An empirical certainty may nevertheless be entitled necessity, namely when for the purposes of the employment of experience it serves as well as a necessity. The former are strict inferences, the latter not strict. In inferences of reason we infer the particular from the universal; in inferences of the understanding, the universal from the particular. The former is *ratiocinium genuinum* [genuine inference], the latter *spurium* [spurious], which the author entitles *ordinaire* [and] *extraordinis*. But since nothing follows from merely particular propositions, the understanding endeavours here to make rules, that is, universal propositions, by means of its perceptions. Thus, for example, I see merely through experience that all men must die, because this man, that man, and all men who have lived have died. Here, then, I infer in the reverse manner from that of *ratiocinia* [inferences of reason]. What belongs to as many individuals as have ever existed belongs to all others of this kind; this is what we entitle induction. This is the path which the understanding continually follows. Here, however, there is no apodictic certainty. For individual cases may still always form exceptions to our so-called rules; for example, some human bodies are still found in which the heart beats on the right side, and in which, in general, everything is reversed. The physician nevertheless [does not on that account alter] [ändert] his *laudana*. The same holds of all languages and of the general character of nations. It is said, for example, that all people in France are polite, only not the innkeepers; and conversely that in England all are rude, but the innkeepers polite. On the whole, the rule *nulla regula sine exceptione* [no rule without exception] belongs here. In disputation these exceptions are entitled instances. The rule then has only an actuality [Wirklichkeit] for inferring in experience, and there may be an exception, provided only that the whole magnitude is not thereby annulled. Through induction we make universal propositions, and this inference is *a particulaire ad vniversale* [from the particular to the universal]. There is also, besides, a kind of induction from systematic propositions, that is, when something is predicated of a genus because it already lies in the analysis of the concept itself; for example, man is rational. There is yet a second way of inferring *oblique* [indirectly], namely through analogy. This mode of inference is distinguished from the preceding as follows: if two things have in common as many features as I have perceived through the most rigorous investigation, they may also agree in all the features which I am not yet able to investigate. I therefore base my inference not on a number of judgments but on properties of these selfsame things. Thus we infer the circulation of the blood in animals from the otherwise similar constitution of the human and the animal body; and likewise the internal structure of polyps, in which we otherwise observe no viscera. These inferences by induction and analogy are, as it were, the two crutches of human ratiocination; true crutches, for in them we go very unsteadily. Hence it is a mere attempt. You say, for example, that all young come into the world either alive or are hatched from eggs. Notwithstanding this, it has always been known that with polyps there are young in every part that is cut off. *Sophisma* is a dialectical mode of inference which has merely the semblance of truth. The semblance may lie",[]],"busolt-s29-032":["a.) In the matter, that is, in the *praemissae* or in the propositions themselves; or",[]],"busolt-s29-033":["b.) In the form, that is, when the semblance lies in the conclusion (in the manner of inferring).",[]],"busolt-s29-034":["Of the former we do not speak here. Some dialecticians amused themselves with the second, others with the first. Such an inference is entitled a paralogistic one when, for example, a term is ambiguous; for example, \"No artist is born.\" The ancients had such an inference, which they entitled the Liar: if you say that you lie and you speak the truth, then you speak the truth when you lie. *Sophisma heterogenea, vbi Status quaestionis mutatur, dicitur etiam Sophisma ignorationis elenchi. Hic pertinet illud alter mulget hircum, alter supponit cribrum* [The heterogeneous sophism, in which the state of the question is changed, is also called the sophism of ignorance of the refutation. Here belongs the saying: one milks the he-goat, the other holds the sieve beneath]. Copulative sophisms are those in which two syllogisms are in fact combined, as: Everything simple is immortal; God and all spirits are simple.",[]],"busolt-s29-035":["The *ars Polysyllogistica* [polysyllogistic art] consists in our linking many inferences together, so that the conclusion of the preceding inference becomes the premiss of the following one. Here we always infer in episyllogisms, and the polysyllogistic is then *progrediens* [progressive]. If, however, I go back to the deduction and prove one of the premisses, the polysyllogistic is *regrediens* [regressive]. The former appears otherwise to be advantageously employed, especially in disputation; but great caution is required in its use. For if in the progressive polysyllogistic I grant the first premiss, and do not properly examine its correctness in matter and form, true things may always be inferred, but they stand and fall with the premiss. In order, then, that no error of this kind should occur in disputation, it is advisable that the conclusion of the opponent should always yield the assertion of the respondent. The error mentioned in progressive syllogisms has not seldom crept into philosophical systems. The regressive syllogistic, on the other hand, has far more advantages than the former; for here I examine critically whether the ground is true and the consequence correct. A *sorites* is merely an abbreviation [Folgerung] of expression. In it I heap conclusion upon conclusion and leave out the premisses. Such a chain of inferences may consist of categorical [or] hypothetical propositions, and so there is a *sorites categoricus* [and a] *sorites hypotheticus*. *Circulus in demonstrando* [circle in demonstrating]. *Saltus in probando* [leap in proving] occurs when I infer *a ratione remota* [from a remote ground] directly *ad rationatum* [to the consequence]. Here, then, the consequence is not clear, and I leave out a necessary intermediate inference.",[]],"busolt-s29-036":["*Petitio principii*. *Petere principium* means to ask for a principle; *postulare principium* is to demand that one be permitted [to assume] what one has to prove. A proof which shows too little is on that account to be regarded not as false but as defective; for I prove something, and something may still always be added. But a proof which proves too much is false and proves nothing at all.",[]],"busolt-s30-001":["The *Topica* of Aristotle were nothing other than a review of all the titles and *argumenta* under which objects can be considered; just as, for example, we can consider a thing from different points of view. A *locus topicus* is a title, alleged in logic, under which an object can be considered and treated. I can consider a thing *in genere* [in its genus] and *in differentia specifica* [in its specific difference]; according to distinctness and indistinctness; as ground and consequence; as axiom and theorem, etc. Thus a metaphysical place, *locus metaphysicus*: *loci rerum sunt etiam fines rerum* [the places of things are also the limits of things]. There are *loci grammatici*; there I can consider an object according to its definition. These *loci logici*, because they concern only the form, are entitled commonplaces [gemeinarten], *loci communes*, or better, common propositions [gemeinsäzze]. *Locorum communium vsus et in logicis et in rhetoricis locum habet* [The use of commonplaces has its place both in logic and in rhetoric]. The *loci ethici* treat of the *decorum* and the *honestum*; the *loci physici* [concern] whether I derive a thing from efficient or from final causes.",[]],"busolt-s31-001":["Just as the theoretical part of logic expounds the universal condition of the possible perfection of logical knowledge, so practical logic is a universal science which contains the condition of attaining to a possible logically perfect knowledge. The question here, then, is: how am I to attain to the possible logical perfection in knowledge, where am I to apply this [perfection], and where that? Here one must beware of tautological definitions. For the solution is usually dispatched thus: one should do what is required for it. It is better to say instead: acquaintance with the object in hand here furnishes the best solution. For in this the matter alone is what counts, and this not even practical logic can have for its object. Only in so far as it belongs to form can it teach us to apply all the rules of the theory; it teaches, therefore, only the application. The chief object of practical logic is accordingly method, that is, the arrangement of the manifold of knowledge with all logical perfection, in so far as it is to be combined into a whole. Practical logic might accordingly also be entitled the pure universal doctrine of method. Method is contradistinguished from manner [Manier]. The latter requires merely a common mode of teaching, and each person therefore has his own manner, his own taste; popularity, however, is the chief feature of it. Manner is a mode of treating the object according to laws of reason. Manner, accordingly, like everything empirical, cannot be brought under rules. Popularity, however, is its most praiseworthy property; for it presupposes logical perfection, since it is nothing other than *accomodatio Logicae cognitionis ad sensum communem* [the accommodation of logical knowledge to common sense]. Here one shows, without any art, how our concepts gradually expand without scholastic rigour. Further, manner and method are distinguished in that method belongs to science, manner merely to entertainment; just as the latter makes itself known through inclination and practice. In the latter even the faults are already present, for it is nature; just as in women even the blemishes on the face make their beauty more prominent.",[]],"busolt-s31-002":["Opposed to method is:",[]],"busolt-s31-003":["a.) The tumultuary, in which no order is observed in thinking. In conceiving [a work], however, the tumultuary is permissible and advantageous;",[]],"busolt-s31-004":["for one looks about, as it were, quite freely in the field of objects; whereas method sets bounds to us in our consideration. For the tumultuary serves manifoldness, method unity. This tumultuary [element] in method is always a proof of a tumultuary way of thinking.",[]],"busolt-s31-005":["b.) There is a kind of exposition which is rhapsodic. In it one may always have observed an order in thinking, only one is not able to make the guiding thread of one's consideration visible. This is revealed especially in the lack of titles for the sections; in short, what is properly due to method is wanting; for that is what makes [the parts] clear to one another.",[]],"busolt-s31-006":["c.) An exposition is entitled aphoristic when method does indeed shine forth in the thinking and even in the exposition, only the transition from one part to another is not distinctly represented; when there is unity in the thinking, but not continuity. The preceding chapter contains, as it were, no propaedeutic for the transition to the following one. The aphorists take us by surprise, as it were, and not seldom become agreeable. A book of rhapsodic method, on the other hand, is intolerable, since one cannot readily look things up in the archive of memory. In the aphoristic method it is, as it were, as though I sprang from one stone to another, the stones lying in a straight line.",[]],"busolt-s31-007":["d.) Finally, the popular method, as the most generally useful, deserves to be preferred to all others. For it is, as it were, the condescension of a man of science to the common human understanding.",[]],"busolt-s31-008":["Method proper demands far too much exertion of the understanding, and it then forbids every kind of reconciliation [Versöhnung]. In popularity the French have the great advantage; a Fontenelle can make a mathematical proposition just as comprehensible as an aesthetic truth. Method is also divided into analytic and synthetic. In the synthetic one proceeds from principles to *principiata*, in the other the reverse; thus the whole of mathematics is synthetic. The analytic is, as it were, an inverse synthesis, and is [the method of] philosophy. Further, method is divided:",[]],"busolt-s31-009":["a.) Into the scientific. This is for scholars, and in it one has regard especially to thoroughness. It expounds everything *in abstracto*; order and distinctness, namely, are the chief concern in it.",[]],"busolt-s31-010":["b.) Into the popular. This has regard also to ideas, in that in it everything is expounded *in concreto*. Thus Hume wrote reflections on the human understanding in the scientific method, but with little approval. He treated the very same matter in the popular method, introducing comparisons drawn from rhetoric, poetry, sensation, etc., and the book met with universal approval. Remark on the foregoing. The synthetic method is suited only to those sciences in which one proceeds from the universal to the particular, that is, to mathematics; whereas in philosophy the analytic is mostly in place, certain parts excepted. The mathematical method is method proper. This same method, applied to other sciences, is merely futile; otherwise one falls into the vice of a mannerism of method [Methodenmanier]. There is further the division of method into:",[]],"busolt-s31-011":["a.) The natural, namely the objectively natural, which is suited to the object. Method *in concreto* is a natural [method].",[]],"busolt-s31-012":["b.) The artificial. If this artificial [method] is to have no fault, it must be nature; for art is in truth nothing other than cultivated nature. This is just what is entitled naivety, namely an art which seems nature, or a nature which seems art. Further, the syllogistic mode of teaching: there is properly no such thing, nor ought it to be tolerated; for whom do formal inferences not vex? Yet with individual propositions, in disputation, it is permissible. The tabular method is that in which one sketches the skeleton of a system. The dialogical method instructs by discoursing. It is twofold:",[]],"busolt-s31-013":["a.) Socratic, in which the teacher poses as a pupil and, by analysis, seeks to have the question decided by the pupil himself. It is so entitled because Socrates made particular use of it in his dialogues.",[]],"busolt-s31-014":["b.) Catechetical. This is merely for modes of knowledge of memory; for in it the teacher himself gives them to the pupil. The geographical method is that which expounds objects that exist at the same time.",[]],"busolt-s31-015":["The chronological method is that which expounds objects that follow one another in time. There is further the division into doctrine and discipline. Doctrine extends our modes of knowledge, and is therefore positive. Discipline merely wards off error, and is therefore negative. Tumultuary meditation has much that is advantageous in it; for through this kind of foray, in which, [straying] from the footpath, we [range] without halting everywhere",[]],"busolt-s31-016":["and plunder from every region, our knowledge certainly gains not a little.",[]],"busolt-s32-001":["In meditating one needs an object; consequently one must gather the materials, and this is best done through tumultuary thinking. For its further promotion one reads [peset] books, especially where historical or polemical matters are concerned; otherwise, too, the thinking of others gives us occasion for new ideas and new thoughts.",[]],"busolt-s32-002":["Lambert used lexicons and sought out synonyms, analogies, etc.; and in this way one attains manifoldness. This one observes in thinking; in writing down, however, unity is required. Subsidiary thoughts which occur to us, as it were, along the way are noted in the margin and afterwards distributed into their proper compartments. But since our imagination, once it has been set on a journey, does not readily leave off from it, one must, in order not always to consider the object from one point of view only, force oneself to break off frequently, to read books on quite different matters, and, as it were, to make a diversion. In the morning one should put the materials for a system in order; in the evening, on the other hand, one should gather materials. For in the evening the imagination roams about, as it were, occupied with many objects; in the morning, however, it is, as it were, under the supervision of reason.",[]],"busolt-s33-001":["Speaking [Sprechen] and discoursing [reden] are distinct. One speaks in order to make oneself understood; one discourses in order to attain one's end. How, then, is the philosopher to order his language? In general the philosopher looks more to the meaning of words than to style. The principle of this is: the expression must be adequate to the concept. He may therefore also use the language of the learned, that is, not only a dead language but also technical terms. These, however, are, so to speak, only a makeshift, and he does not depart from common usage without deliberation and necessity; yet for philosophical principles a language is seldom sufficient in respect of expression. German appears, along with Greek, to be the most philosophical; for it is the most correct of all, and has the most synonyms. Notwithstanding this, one seldom finds two words in the whole language which are identical; for the multitude of synonyms is always a proof of the richness of a language, since by means of synonyms one can determine the different degrees of exactness in the object. The cause of the richness of the German language is fittingly traced to its missionaries, who, when they sought to convert [the Germans], formed many of their already invented words after the Greek and Latin, as *Mitleiden* from *compassio*, *Barmherzigkeit* from *misericordia*. Precisely because these missionaries, all of them improvers of the German language, wished to enlighten the understanding of the Germans, they had also to speak with few tropes; and hence they were obliged of necessity to enrich the foreign [Welsche] language with entirely new words, since otherwise these very tropes are a proof of the poverty of a language. When a language has many tropes, this gives occasion to various plays on words and witty conceits.",[]],"busolt-s33-002":["Coining words [Wortschneiderei] is a great fault. It is indeed said *verba valent sicut nummi* [words have currency like coins]; but it is only the supreme authority of the land that has the right of coinage. For the rest, it remains true [that]",[]],"busolt-s33-003":["*Penes vsum est et ius et norma loquendi* [With usage lies both the right and the rule of speaking]",[]],"busolt-s33-004":["*Finis.*",[]]};
